IS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2012).  aFeatured between 2009-01 and 2012-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aContinued by a successor resource (linked below).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.  aPublished by the Institute of Museum and Library Services (IMLS), this website is the online companion to the IMLS “Connecting to Collections Bookshelf”, a core set of books, DVDs, online resources, and an annotated bibliography that is being distributed free to nearly 3,000 collecting institutions. According to the site, “The Guide contains links to the most trusted collections care resources on the Web. Use it to find answers to common conservation and collections management questions.”cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aMuseum conservation methods.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85088711 0aCollection management (Libraries)0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh96004573 0aArchival materialsxConservation and restoration.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh97007224 0aLaw librarianship, cataloging & information management 0aArt & cultural heritage 6aPortal or gateway (links to other sites) 7aAnnotated bibliographies.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20190260152 aInstitute of Museum and Library Services (U.S.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9606106300tConnecting to Collections Care (C2C Care)423Successor resource: Connecting to Collections Care (C2C Care)uhttps://connectingtocollections.org423Successor resource, archived version: Connecting to Collections Care (C2C Care)uhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261029033950/https://connectingtocollections.orgzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.423Successor resource: C2C Care at FAIC/AICuhttps://www.culturalheritage.org/c2ccare423Successor resource, archived version: C2C Care at FAIC/AICuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250426175300/https://www.culturalheritage.org/c2ccarezBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.04125nai a22005417i wotm2012-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)291100270  an-us---aa-iq---00aIraq War :b2003 Web Archive. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bLibrary of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2012).  aFeatured between 2009-01 and 2012-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aSubject headings from the University of Colorado Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aPart of the Library of Congress Web Archives, this archive contains selective collection of 231 Web sites, archived beginning on March 13, 2003 related to the Iraq War. Included in the archive are websites from the U.S. and foreign governments, public policy and advocacy groups, educational organizations, religious organizations, support groups for military personnel, anti-war groups, sites that target children, and news sources. The Iraq War Web archive consists of three phases of collection: the first phase, a weekly capture, began on March 13, 2003 with the commencement of the war and ended June 30, 2003. Phase 1 has been processed and is available from this site. Phase 2 is a weekly capture and covers December 2003 to December 2004. Phase 3, also a weekly capture, was begun in January 2005 and is ongoing (archives from these later phases are not yet available).cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aIraq War, 2003-2011.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2003003059 0aAfghan War, 2001-2021.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2004004460 0aVeteranszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh86005596 0aIraq War, 2003-2011xForeign public opinion. 0aIraq War, 2003-2011xMass media and the war.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008123895 0aIraq War, 2003-2011xMoral and ethical aspects.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2009127543 0aIraq War, 2003-2011xPolitical aspects. 0aIraq War, 2003-2011xProtest movements. 0aIraq War, 2003-2011xPublic opinion. 0aIraq War, 2003-2011xCasualties. 0aWeb archives.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2004001365 0aIraqxHistoryy2003-0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2004001802 0aUnited StatesxMilitary policy.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85140379 0aUnited StatesxPolitics and governmenty2001-2009.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2001002071 0aUnited StatesxSocial policyy1993-0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh92006374 0aNational security, military & emergency law 0aUnited States (federal) 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aIraq 6aDigital archive / collection2 aLibrary of Congress,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7808903540uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/iraq-war-2003-web-archive/about-this-collection/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260204042044/https://www.loc.gov/collections/iraq-war-2003-web-archive/about-this-collection/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02540nai a22004097i wotm2012-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)772622123  aa-cc---00aChina Guiding Cases Project. 1a[Stanford, Calif.] :bStanford Law School  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2012).  aFeatured between 2009-01 and 2012-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aSubject headings from the University of Colorado Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe China Guiding Cases Project (CGCP) is an initiative of Stanford Law School to advance knowledge and understanding of Chinese law, and to enable judges and legal experts both inside and outside of China to contribute to the evolution of Chinese case law through ongoing dialogue on guiding cases released by the Supreme People’s Court of the People’s Republic of China.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.10aChina.bZui gao ren min fa yuan.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n878917751http://isni.org/isni/0000000115037867 0aJudicial opinionszChina. 0aLawzChinaxInterpretation and construction. 0aJudge-made lawzChina. 0aJudicial processzChina. 0aForeign & comparative law 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aChina 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102617240uhttps://law.stanford.edu/china-guiding-cases-project/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260122071407/https://law.stanford.edu/china-guiding-cases-project/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03085nai a22003617i wotm2012-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| d o    i0    2eng d00aDoing Business :bMeasuring Business Regulations. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bWorld Bank Group  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2012).  aFeatured between 2009-01 and 2012-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aPublished by the International Finance Corporation, a member of the World Bank Group, this database provides objective measures of business regulations and their enforcement across 181 economies and selected cities at the subnational and regional level. They indicate the regulatory costs of business and can be used to analyse specific regulations that enhance or constrain investment, productivity and growth. The indicators are developed by the Private Sector Vice Presidency of the World Bank Group in cooperation with the Lex Mundi Association of law firms and the International Bar Association. The principal data collection methods for the indicators are the study of the existing laws and regulations in each economy; targeted interviews with regulators, or, private sector professionals in each topic; and cooperative arrangements with other departments of the World Bank, other donor agencies, private consulting firms, business and law associations.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCommercial law.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85028940 0aIndustrial laws and legislation.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85065887 0aEconomic indicators.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85040831 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aForeign & comparative law 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aStatistics.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261812 aWorld Bank,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7904340340uhttps://archive.doingbusiness.org/en/doingbusiness413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261013014401/https://archive.doingbusiness.org/en/doingbusinesszBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02428nai a22003617i wotm2012-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  an-us---00aJoint Committee on Taxation. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bUnited States Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2012).  aFeatured between 2009-01 and 2012-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis is the official website of the Joint Committee on Taxation, U.S. Congress. The website contains all Committee publications from 1920. It also provides access to the Committee’s rules, history, and membership list. Many thanks to Ms. Victoria Sukhol, Assistant Director for Cataloging at the New York Law School, who suggested and cataloged this website.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aUnited States. Congress. Joint Committee on Taxation.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79021613 0aTaxationxLaw and legislationzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008112540 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website 7aLegislative materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263621 aUnited States.bCongress.bJoint Committee on Taxation,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7902161340uhttps://www.jct.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261005105559/https://www.jct.gov/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03043nai a22003737i wotm2012-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  an-us---00aWall Street and the Financial Collapse :bAnatomy of a Financial Crisis.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aWall Street and the Financial Crisis: Anatomy of a Financial Collapse: Majority and Minority Staff Report 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bUnited States Senate. Majority and Minority Staff Report.  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2012).  aFeatured between 2009-01 and 2012-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aPrepared by the Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations under the Senate Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs, this report examines the various factors contributing to the financial crisis, such as high risk lending, regulatory failure, inflated credit ratings, and investment bank abuses. It contains case studies on Washington Mutual Bank, Office of Thrift Supervision, Moody’s and Standards & Poor’s, Goldman Sachs and Deutsche Bank.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aGlobal Financial Crisis, 2008-2009.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2009003683 0aFinancial criseszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008120571 0aBanks and bankingzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85011640 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aUnited States (federal) 6aReport or study (single document) 7aLegislative materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263621 aUnited States.bCongress.bSenate.bCommittee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs.bPermanent Subcommittee on Investigations,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n200502580940uhttps://www.hsgac.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/imo/media/doc/Financial_Crisis/FinancialCrisisReport.pdf413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260828183007/https://www.hsgac.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/imo/media/doc/Financial_Crisis/FinancialCrisisReport.pdfzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02597nai a22003377i wotm2012-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| d o    i0    2eng d00aUnited Nations Legal Publications :bGlobal Search. 1a[New York] :bUnited Nations  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2012).  aFeatured between 2009-01 and 2012-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe United Nations Global Search engine provides a cross-database search option for researchers interested in easily locating multiple types of UN legal publications pertaining to a particular topic. Publications available for search include: Yearbook of the International Law Commission; UNCITRAL yearbook; United Nations juridical yearbook; UNCITRAL publications; Reports of international arbitral awards; Repertory of Practice of United Nations Organs; Summaries of Judgments, Advisory Opinions and Orders of the International Court of Justice; Repertoire of Practice of the Security Council; and Proceedings of Diplomatic Conferences.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aUnited Nations.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79021345 0aInternational lawvDatabases. 0aInternational law & organizations 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260812 aUnited Nations.bOffice of Legal Affairs,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8012865640uhttps://legal.un.org/globalsearch/dtSearch/Search_Forms/dtSearch.html413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260715104054/https://legal.un.org/globalsearch/dtSearch/Search_Forms/dtSearch.htmlzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02772nai a22003857i wotm2012-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)341769831  an-us---04aThe September 11 Digital Archive :bSaving the Histories of September 11, 2001. 1a[New York] :bAmerican Social History Project  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2012).  aFeatured between 2009-01 and 2012-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aFunded by a major grant from the Alfred P. Sloan Foundation and organized by the American Social History Project at the City University of New York Graduate Center and the Center for History and New Media at George Mason University, the Archive contributes to the on-going effort by historians and archivists to record and preserve the record of 9/11 by collecting and archiving first-hand accounts, emails and other electronic communications, digital photographs and artworks, and a range of other digital materials related to the attacks.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aSeptember 11 Terrorist Attacks, 2001.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2001000147 0aSeptember 11 Terrorist Attacks, 2001vArchives. 0aTerrorismzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2001009946 0aNational security, military & emergency law 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection2 aAmerican Social History Project,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n882924122 aRoy Rosenzweig Center for History and New Media,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201206054840uhttps://911digitalarchive.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261026231744/https://911digitalarchive.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03095nai a22003857i wotm2013-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuudcu|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)75232197400aGender Jurisprudence and International Criminal Law Project. 1aWashington, DC :bAmerican University Washington College of Law  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013, 2014).  aFeatured between 2013-06 and 2013-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aFeatured between 2014-01 and 2014-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aOriginal site no longer available (checked 2026-11-10).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Gender Jurisprudence and International Criminal Law Project is a collaborative project between the War Crimes Research Office (WCRO) and the Women and International Law Program (WILP) at the American University Washington College of Law. The goal of the project is to raise awareness of and facilitate discourse about gender-based crimes committed during times of conflict, mass violence, or repression, and the prosecution of these crimes under international law.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aThe Gender Jurisprudence Collections (GJC) is a powerful database containing judgments, decisions, orders, and other relevant documents issued by international/ized criminal courts and tribunals that have been coded and made readily searchable for issues relating to sexual and gender-based violence.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aWar crime trialsvDatabases. 0aInternational criminal courtsvDatabases. 0aInternational criminal law & transitional justice 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026172403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250209175716/https://cear-euskadi.org/guia/en/gender-jurisprudence-and-international-criminal-law-project-american-university-washington-college-of-law/zArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.02414nai a22003497i wotm2013-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| d o    |0    2eng d  an-us---00aWomen’s Legal History. 1a[Stanford, California] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2013-05 and 2013-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Women’s Legal History website is the home of a searchable database of articles and papers on pioneering women lawyers in the United States. In collaboration with Professor Babcock and her students, the Robert Crown Library Staff have created this website as a resource for all who are interested in the subject of women lawyers in the United States. The main tool is the study of individual lives and of the movements and philosophies that inspired and sustained them. Thanks to Ismael Gullon who suggested this site!cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aWomen lawyerszUnited StatesxHistory. 0aWomen lawyerszUnited StatesvBiography.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2010119089 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aUnited States (federal) 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402608140uhttps://wlh.law.stanford.edu413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261012045100/https://wlh.law.stanford.eduzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03005nai a22003857i wotm2013-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999sz || d o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)4584797200aECOLEX. 1a[Gland, Switzerland] :bThe World Conservation Union  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2013-04 and 2013-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aSubject headings from the University of Colorado Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aECOLEX is an information service on environmental law, operated jointly by FAO, IUCN and UNEP. Its purpose is to build capacity worldwide by providing the most comprehensive possible global source of information on environmental law. This resource, which combines the environmental law information holdings of FAO, IUCN and UNEP, seeks to put this information at the disposal of users world-wide, in an easily accessible service, employing modern technology. The ECOLEX database includes information on treaties, international soft-law and other non-binding policy and technical guidance documents, national legislation, judicial decisions, and law and policy literature. Users have direct access to the abstracts and indexing information about each document, as well as to the full text of most of the information provided.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aEnvironmental law, InternationalvDatabases. 0aEnvironmental lawvDatabases. 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aTreaties.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110267072 aInternational Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n790584202 aUnited Nations Environment Programme,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8000238840uhttps://www.ecolex.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261020063657/https://www.ecolex.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03054nai a22004217i wotm2013-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)43323366  an-us---00aCenter on Budget and Policy Priorities. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bCenter on Budget and Policy Priorities  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2013-03 and 2013-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aSubject headings from the University of Colorado Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Center on Budget and Policy Priorities is a nonpartisan research organization and policy institute that conducts research and analysis on a range of government policies and programs, with an emphasis on those affecting low and moderate income people. The Center conducts research to help shape public debates over proposed budget and tax policies and to help ensure that policymakers consider the needs of low-income families and individuals in these debates. In addition, the Center examines the short- and long-term impacts of proposed policies on the health of the economy and the soundness of federal and state budgets.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aCenter on Budget and Policy Priorities (Washington, D.C.)0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n868219881https://isni.org/isni/000000012220394X 0aEconomic assistance, DomesticzUnited States.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85040782 0aGovernment spending policyzUnited States.0https://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85056109 0aPovertyxGovernment policyzUnited States. 0aPovertyxResearchzUnited States. 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aOrganization website2 aCenter on Budget and Policy Priorities (Washington, D.C.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8682198840uhttps://www.cbpp.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261013171057/https://www.cbpp.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03603nai a22004097i wotm2013-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xx || w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)794010796  an-us---00aLocal Government Ethics Programs :bA Resource for Ethics Commission Members, Local Officials, Attorneys, Journalists, and Students, And A Manual for Ethics Reform.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aLocal Government Ethics Programs: A Resource for Ethics Commission Members, Ethics Reformers, Local Officials, Attorneys, Journalists, and Students 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bWechsler, Robert  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2013-02 and 2013-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aSubject headings from the University of Colorado Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aAs stated on its website, this book is intended as an introduction to local government ethics for those who want to have a grasp on the various issues involved in government ethics; as a resource for those faced with a specific situation, who want to understand the legal and ethical issues involved, as well as the alternative ways of dealing with the situation; as a guidebook for those involved in government ethics reform; and as a text for students of public administration as well as supplementary reading for local public administrators.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLocal governmentxLaw and legislationzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2010100052 0aLocal governmentxMoral and ethical aspectszUnited States. 0aLocal officials and employeesxProfessional ethicszUnited States. 0aPolitical ethicszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008109553 0aElections, ethics & government accountability 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aHandbooks and manuals.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402610940uhttps://www.cityethics.org/files/lgep1-0.-.Robert.Wechsler.pdf413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20121115065750/http://www.cityethics.org/files/lgep1-0%20-%20Robert%20Wechsler.pdfzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.423About this resource (not the resource itself): Author's announcement of the book, City Ethics blog, 2012-05-08uhttps://www.cityethics.org/articles/my-book-is-finally-available-and-its-free423About this resource (not the resource itself), archived version: Author's announcement of the book, City Ethics blog, 2012-05-08uhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261110210901/https://www.cityethics.org/articles/my-book-is-finally-available-and-its-freezBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02776nai a22004097i wotm2013-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)793012989  aa-af---00aAfghanistan Documentation Project. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bAmerican University Washington College of Law  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2013-01 and 2013-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aSubject headings from the University of Colorado Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Afghanistan Documentation Project is the product of a partnership between the War Crimes Research Office and the Pence Law Library of the American University Washington College of Law and the U.S. Institute of Peace. It was established to collect and create a fully searchable and publicly accessible database of documents regarding human rights and humanitarian law violations committed in Afghanistan since 1978.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aHuman rightszAfghanistanvDatabases. 0aHuman rightszAfghanistanvSourcesvDatabases. 0aAtrocitieszAfghanistanvDatabases. 0aInsurgencyzAfghanistanvDatabases. 0aViolencezAfghanistanvDatabases. 0aHuman rights 0aInternational criminal law & transitional justice 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aAfghanistan 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402608140uhttps://www.wcl.american.edu/impact/initiatives-programs/warcrimes/our-projects/afghanistan-documentation-project/?previewid=34098B32-057A-81EB-4E6F8B012814BBEE413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250725012848/https://www.wcl.american.edu/impact/initiatives-programs/warcrimes/our-projects/afghanistan-documentation-project/?previewid=34098B32-057A-81EB-4E6F8B012814BBEEzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02725nai a22003617i wotm2013-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  an-us---00aLegal Issues in Mass Digitization :bA Preliminary Analysis and Discussion Document.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aLegal Issues in Mass Digitization: Preliminary Analysis and Discussion Document 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bUnited States Copyright Office  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2012-12 and 2013-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Library of Congress Copyright Office published a preliminary analysis and discussion document that addresses the issues raised by the intersection between copyright law and the mass digitization of books. The purpose of the analysis is to facilitate further discussions among the affected parties and the public discussions that may encompass a number of possible approaches, including voluntary initiatives, legislative options, or both. The document also identifies questions to consider in determining an appropriate policy for the mass digitization of books.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCopyrightzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85032489 0aCopyright and digital preservationzUnited States. 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 0aLaw librarianship, cataloging & information management 0aUnited States (federal) 6aReport or study (single document)2 aLibrary of Congress.bCopyright Office,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7911797140uhttps://copyright.gov/docs/massdigitization/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261012150623/https://copyright.gov/docs/massdigitization/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03049nai a22004217i wotm2013-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)62246163  an-us---00aHomeland security digital library. 1aMonterey, CA :bNaval Postgraduate School Center for Homeland Defense and Security  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2012-11 and 2013-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.1 aAccess restricted to subscribersfOnline access with authorization2star  aThe Homeland Security Digital Library (HSDL) is a collection of documents related to homeland security policy, strategy, and organizational management. It is sponsored by the U.S. Department of Homeland Security’s National Preparedness Directorate, FEMA and the Naval Postgraduate School Center for Homeland Defense and Security. The documents are collected from a wide variety of sources, including federal, state, tribal, and local government agencies, professional organizations, think tanks, academic institutions, and international governing bodies. Please note that access to some content is restricted to Federal depository libraries and other users with valid user accounts.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aNaval Postgraduate School (U.S.) Center for Homeland Defense and SecurityxResearch. 0aNational securityxResearchzUnited States. 0aCivil defensexResearchzUnited States. 0aCivil defensexResearch. 0aMilitary readinessxResearch. 0aNational securityxResearch. 0aNational security, military & emergency law 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection2 aNaval Postgraduate School (U.S.). Center for Homeland Defense and Security,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no20050776611 aUnited States.bDepartment of Homeland Security,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200205173040uhttps://www.hsdl.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261021202349/https://www.hsdl.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02478nai a22003737i wotm2013-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nbu|| w o    s0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)807809356  an-us-dc00aCivil War Washington. 1a[Lincoln, Nebraska] :bCenter for Digital Research in the Humanities  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2012-10 and 2012-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aSubject headings from the University of Colorado Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aCivil War Washington examines the U.S. national capital from multiple perspectives as a case study of social, political, cultural, and medical/scientific transitions provoked or accelerated by the Civil War. The project draws on the methods of many fields–literary studies, history, geography, computer-aided mapping–to create a digital resource that chronicles the war’s impact on the city.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aUnited StatesxHistoryyCivil War, 1861-1865.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85140205 0aWashington (D.C.)xHistoryy19th century. 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 5aDistrict of Columbia 6aDigital archive / collection2 aUniversity of Nebraska--Lincoln.bCenter for Digital Research in the Humanities,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200806037440uhttps://civilwardc.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260921123116/https://civilwardc.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02722nai a22003737i wotm2013-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  an-us---00aLegislative History Research :bA Basic Guide. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bTaylor, Julia  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2012-09 and 2012-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe first report is published by the Congressional Research Service. It provides an overview of federal legislative history research, the legislative process, and where to find congressional documents. The report also summarizes some of the reasons researchers are interested in legislative histories, briefly describes the actions a piece of legislation might undergo during the legislative process, and provides a list of easily accessible print and electronic resources.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLegislative historieszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2009129488 0aLegal researchzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008106837 0aLegislationzUnited States. 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aUnited States (federal) 6aReport or study (single document) 7aGuidebooks.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261082 aLibrary of Congress.bCongressional Research Service,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7906132140uhttps://www.everycrsreport.com/files/20110615_R41865_43b51f010c39e2411ad889a1e7d2d94dce3a7730.html413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250206123317/https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/20110615_R41865_43b51f010c39e2411ad889a1e7d2d94dce3a7730.htmlzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02991nai a22003977i wotm2013-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999ohu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)799083301  an-us---00aPrint Management at “Mega-scale” :bA Regional Perspective on Print Book Collections in North America. 1a[Dublin, Ohio] :bLavoie, Brian  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2012-08 and 2012-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aSubject headings from the University of Colorado Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe second report is published by OCLC Research. Using a mega-regions (“geographical regions defined on the basis of economic integration and other forms of interdependence”) framework to model regional consolidation of shared print book library collections, this report explores a counterfactual scenario where local US and Canadian print book library collections are consolidated into regional shared collections. The analysis in this paper builds upon findings from the OCLC Research report, Cloud-sourcing Research Collections: Managing Print in the Mass-digitized Library Environment (2011), and draws upon bibliographic and library holdings data from the WorldCat database.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLibrary materialszUnited StatesvStatistics. 0aLibrary materialszCanadavStatistics. 0aMetropolitan areaszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85084452 0aMetropolitan areaszCanada. 0aCooperative collection development (Libraries)zUnited States. 0aCooperative collection development (Libraries)zCanada. 0aLaw librarianship, cataloging & information management 6aReport or study (single document)2 aOCLC Research,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201007751640uhttps://www.oclc.org/research/publications/2012/2012-05r.html413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260606015433/https://www.oclc.org/research/publications/2012/2012-05r.htmlzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02433nai a22003617i wotm2013-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuuxx || w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)794135298  an-us---04aThe Sustainable 21st Century Law Library :bVision, Deployment and Assessment for Access to Justice. 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bZorza, Richard  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2013).  aFeatured between 2012-07 and 2012-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aOriginal site no longer available (checked 2026-11-10).  aSubject headings from the University of Colorado Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis report is prepared by Richard Zorza, and co-sponsored by the University of Hawaii School of Law and the Access to Justice Commissions from several states. The author points out that law libraries can transform themselves as leaders in providing access to justice to all, and discusses the range of changes that are needed to empower law libraries and their staff to make this transformation.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLaw librarieszUnited StatesxAdministration. 0aLaw librarieszUnited StatesxForecasting. 0aLaw librariesxReference serviceszUnited States. 0aLaw librarianship, cataloging & information management 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 6aReport or study (single document)403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20131208042012/http://www.zorza.net/LawLibrary.pdfzArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.02593nai a22003497i wotm2014-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)742378613  an-us---00aLegislative histories. 1aWashington, D.C :bUnited States Department of Justice  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2014-06 and 2014-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aOver the years, the U.S. Department of Justice’s (DOJ) library staff has created a prodigious number of legislative histories that encompass a range of legal issues. Originally, these paper volumes were available only to DOJ employees through the Department’s Main Library Collection. Now, these items are freely available for anyone with a penchant for such matters. These exhaustive reports cover two dozen well-known public laws, including the Captive Wildlife Safety Act, the Anti Car Theft Act of 1992, and the Dent Act of 1919. Visitors can scan through each history at their leisure and they can search each document as well.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLegislative historieszUnited States. 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (federal) 7aLegislative histories.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263611 aUnited States.bDepartment of Justice,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7903841940uhttps://www.justice.gov/jmd/ls/legislative-histories413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261025013735/https://www.justice.gov/jmd/ls/legislative-historieszBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03080nai a22003977i wotm2014-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuumau|| w o    |0    2eng d  ae-uk---an-us---00aAppeals to the Privy Council from the American Colonies :bAn annotated digital catalogue. 1a[Cambridge, Massachusetts] :bBilder, Mary Sarah  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2014-05 and 2014-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aNo verified current address for the original site could be found.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aIn the century before the creation of the Supreme Court of the United States, the British Privy Council heard appeals from the 13 colonies that became the United States and from the other ‘American’ colonies in Canada and the Caribbean. This catalogue focuses on all currently known colonial cases appealed to the Privy Council from the future United States, a number totaling nearly one-third of the more than 800 heard from the Americas. For the appeals from the 13 colonies, the catalogue provides links to original documents in England and the United States. Most significantly, the site includes images of surviving briefs filed in 54 of these appeals. Known as ‘printed cases’, these briefs provide the ‘reasons’ for the appeals.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aGreat Britain. Privy Council.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n80050329 0aAppellate procedurezGreat BritainxHistory. 0aLawzUnited StatesxHistory.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85075195 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aGreat Britain (Privy Council 5aAmerican colonies) 6aDigital archive / collection 7aRecords and briefs.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110265242 aAmes Foundation,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n88070930403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260308045315/https://amesfoundation.law.harvard.edu/ColonialAppeals/zArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.02826nai a22003497i wotm2014-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)881434005  an-us---00aLincoln and the law. 1aWashington, DC :bLaw Library of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2014-04 and 2014-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aLincoln’s effort to restore the Union and his contributions to American political thought and its ideals of freedom. Lincoln himself admitted his ambition lay in politics and not in the law, stating “my forte is as a Statesman, rather than a Prosecutor.” Even if the law was Lincoln’s “secondary” avocation, it was indelibly linked to him in life and death. The Law Library of Congress’s historical collection vividly illustrates three periods in which the law played a prominent part of the Lincoln era: first, works specifically on his work as a prominent Illinois lawyer; second, contemporary literature on Lincoln’s controversial balancing of civil liberties against the demands of war aims; third, period transcripts and reports of the trial of the surviving conspirators in the murder of the President and attempted murder of other public officials.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aUnited StatesxPolitics and governmenty1861-1865. 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection2 aLaw Library of Congress (U.S.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n9104313740uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/lincoln-and-the-law/about-this-collection/#Lincoln413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20251025054802/https://www.loc.gov/collections/lincoln-and-the-law/about-this-collection/#LincolnzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02572nai a22003857i wotm2014-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)88014738500aPiracy trials. 1aWashington, DC :bLaw Library of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2014-03 and 2014-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe piracy trials digital library provides access to 57 works pertaining to piracy dated from 1696 through 1905 in PDF format. Included in the collection are several dramatic declarations and confessions of pirates sentenced to death. These publications are helpful for studying piracy, international law, criminal law, and journalism, as well as the law of the American colonies, the United States, Scotland, England, Jamaica, Brazil, Portugal, Germany, and France.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aPiracyxLaw and legislation. 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection 7aTrial and arbitral proceedings.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110267082 aLaw Library of Congress (U.S.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n9104313740uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/piracy-trials/about-this-collection/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260729031557/https://www.loc.gov/collections/piracy-trials/about-this-collection/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03229nai a22004097i wotm2014-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)872150712  an-us---00aAmerican Indian constitutions and legal materials. 1aWashington, DC :bLaw Library of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2014-02 and 2014-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Law Library of Congress holds most of the laws and constitutions from the early nineteenth century produced by the Cherokee, Choctaw, Chickasaw, Creek, and Seminole who were forced to leave the Southeast for the Indian Territory after passage of the Indian Removal Act in 1830. Some of these documents are in the vernacular languages of the tribes. The recognition of Indian nations or tribes by the federal government formally began with the 1934 Indian Reorganization Act. Today, there are some 566 federally recognized sovereign Indian nations and tribes operating under constitutions and charters within the U.S. federal and state structure. The constitutional organization of tribal entities is expressed in the corporate component such as Community, Association, or Community Association, Native Village, Traditional Council, Village of Council, or Corporation added to the name of the tribe.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aIndians of North AmericaxLegal status, laws, etc. 0aIndians of North AmericaxPolitics and government. 0aConstitutionszUnited States. 0aBy-lawszUnited States. 0aIndigenous peoples & tribal law 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aTribal / Indigenous nations 6aDigital archive / collection 7aConstitutions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261672 aLaw Library of Congress (U.S.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n9104313740uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/native-american-constitutions-and-legal-materials/about-this-collection/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260729031337/https://www.loc.gov/collections/native-american-constitutions-and-legal-materials/about-this-collection/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02845nai a22003497i wotm2014-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999sz || d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)70680488500aCustomary IHL. 1aGeneva, Switzerland :bInternational Committee of the Red Cross  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2013-12 and 2014-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aCustomary international humanitarian law (IHL) is important in today’s armed conflicts because it fills gaps left by treaty law in both international and non-international conflicts and so strengthens the protection offered to victims. Mandated by the International Conference of the Red Cross and Red Crescent in 1995. This study identified 161 rules of customary IHL and compiled relevant national and international practice. In 2007, the ICRC teamed up with the British Red Cross to update the practice section of the customary IHL study. Since its inception in 2010, the number of users of the customary IHL database has grown markedly. The collection of practice on the database is an invaluable resource to academics, military advisors and other specialists involved in the practical application of IHL and for any further review of state practice to assess developments in customary IHL.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aHumanitarian lawvDatabases. 0aInternational law & organizations 0aNational security, military & emergency law 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260812 aInternational Committee of the Red Cross,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8015861240uhttps://ihl-databases.icrc.org/en/customary-ihl413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20160405041357/http://www.icrc.org/customary-ihl/eng/docs/homezBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02951nai a22003737i wotm2014-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    |0    2eng d  an-us---00aSexual Assault in the Military :bUnderstanding the Problem and How to Fix It. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2013-11 and 2014-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe military justice system is similar to but distinctly different from its civilian cousin, and it revolves around the concept of enforcing good order and discipline in the armed forces. Arbitrarily taking commanders out of the business of enforcing good order and discipline within their ranks is not the solution to bettering the military’s criminal justice system. Rather, the prudent way to improve the military justice system is to build upon the current system, adopt those policies that enhance the delivery of services to victims and defendants alike, and develop career litigation tracks for military prosecutors and defense counsel.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aSex crimeszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008111570 0aMilitary offenseszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85085251 0aMilitary lawzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85085229 0aNational security, military & emergency law 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aUnited States (federal) 6aReport or study (single document)2 aHeritage Foundation (Washington, D.C.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8317841840uhttps://heritage.org/defense/report/sexual-assault-the-military-understanding-the-problem-and-how-fix-it413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260601200718/https://heritage.org/defense/report/sexual-assault-the-military-understanding-the-problem-and-how-fix-itzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03075nai a22004097i wotm2014-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuusa || d o    |0    2eng d  af-sa---00aLaws of South Africa. 1a[Pretoria] :bSouth Africa  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2013-10 and 2013-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aOriginal site no longer available (checked 2026-11-10).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis database includes all updated South African acts with their rules and regulations (1970- ) as well as all the significant principal acts, rules and regulations from 1910 to the present. The Oliver R Tambo Law Library, University of Pretoria, South Africa, in partnership with SAFLII (South African Legal Information Institute) and the South African Constitutional Court Trust, have undertaken a project to consolidate the South African legislation (the Acts from Parliament) and to supply this information free to the public. At this stage the website is a work in progress and does not yet contain all the Acts.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzSouth Africa. 0aLegislationzSouth Africa. 0aForeign & comparative law 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aSouth Africa 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aStatutes and codes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110266112 aUniversity of Pretoria,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7903441000tSAFLII South Africa: Consolidated Acts403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20241114184903/http://www.lawsofsouthafrica.up.ac.za/zArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.423Successor resource: SAFLII South Africa: Consolidated Actsuhttps://www.saflii.org/za/legis/consol_act/toc-C.html423Successor resource, archived version: SAFLII South Africa: Consolidated Actsuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260223154633/https://www.saflii.org/za/legis/consol_act/toc-C.htmlzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02417nai a22003737i wotm2014-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999txu|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)85967840700aConstitute :bthe world's constitutions to read, search and compare.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aConstitute 1aAustin, Texas? :bComparative Constitutions Project  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2013-09 and 2013-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aConstitute offers access to the world’s constitutions that users can systematically compare them across a broad set of topics – using a modern, clean interface. New constitutions are written every year. The people who write these important documents need to read and analyze texts from other places. Constitute includes the constitution that was in force for nearly every independent state in the world.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aConstitutionsvDatabases. 0aConstitutional lawvDatabases. 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aForeign & comparative law 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aConstitutions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102616740uhttps://www.constituteproject.org/#/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261109234821/https://www.constituteproject.org/#/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02776nai a22003497i wotm2014-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999ne || d o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)37078584500aLegal tools database. 1aHague, The Netherlands :bICC, Office of the Prosecutor  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2013-08 and 2013-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aICC Legal Tools Database serves as an electronic library on international criminal law and justice. It is a work in progress database, and new documents have been publicly available through this web site. They are ICC Documents, ICC ‘Preparatory Works’ and Rome Statute Amendments, International Legal Instruments, International(ised) Criminal Jurisdictions, National Jurisdictions, National Cases Involving Core International Crimes, Internet Legal Resources, Human Rights Decisions, United Nations War Crimes Commission and more. You will find more detailed information on the database at http://www.legal-tools.org/en/overview-of-the-tools/. The search engine on this website is being continuously developed. Feedback on how it can be improved is welcome and should be directed to info@casematrixnetwork.org .cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCriminal jurisdictionvDatabases. 0aCriminal procedure (International law)vDatabases. 0aInternational criminal law & transitional justice 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260812 aInternational Criminal Court.bOffice of the Prosecutor,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201015824240uhttps://www.legal-tools.org/en413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20240218153743/https://www.legal-tools.org/enzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03549nai a22004097i wotm2014-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuudcu|| d o    f0    2eng d  an-us---00aFOIAonline. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2014).  aFeatured between 2013-07 and 2013-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aOriginal site no longer available (checked 2026-11-10).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aFOIAonline is a FOIA tracking and processing tool for the following Agencies and Offices: Department of Commerce (except the US Patent and Trademark Office), Environmental Protection Agency, Federal Labor Relations Authority, Merit Systems Protection Board, National Archives and Records Administration Office of General Counsel. As of December 10, 2012, the Department of the Treasury implemented FOIAonline in a limited capacity. FOIAonline allows users to: request information under FOIA from participating agencies, track the status of your requests, file appeals (registered users only), and search for other people’s requests, appeals and responsive records.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aFreedom of informationzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008103817 0aPublic recordsxAccess controlzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2007009024 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260811 aUnited States.bEnvironmental Protection Agency,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7907959700tFOIA.gov National FOIA Portal403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20180124224418/https://foiaonline.regulations.gov/foia/action/public/homezArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.423Successor resource: FOIA.gov National FOIA Portaluhttps://www.foia.gov423Successor resource, archived version: FOIA.gov National FOIA Portaluhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261109050643/https://www.foia.govzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.423Comparable current resource: FOIAonline backup archiveuhttps://www.muckrock.com/news/archives/2023/oct/25/foiaonline-backup-pogo-muckrock-archive/423Comparable current resource, archived version: FOIAonline backup archiveuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260219013110/https://www.muckrock.com/news/archives/2023/oct/25/foiaonline-backup-pogo-muckrock-archive/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02326nai a22003377i wotm2015-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    |0    2eng d  an-us---00aHuman Rights Campaign :bWorking for Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual and Transgender Equal Rights. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured 2015-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Human Rights Campaign (HRC) represents a force of more than 1.5 million members and commits to making HRC’s vision a reality. HRC envisions a world where lesbian, gay, bisexual and transgender (LGBT) people are ensured equality and embraced as full members of society at home, at work and in every community. The Campaign promotes public education and welfare for the lesbian, gay, bisexual and transgender community and strives to end discrimination against LGBT people and to install fairness and equality for all.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aGay rightszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008105072 0aSexual minoritiesxLegal status, laws, etc.zUnited States. 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 6aOrganization website40uhttps://www.hrc.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20200101064051/https://www.hrc.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02489nai a22003737i wotm2015-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)41424166  an------acl-----00aInter-American Commission on Human Rights (Website). 1aWashington, D.C :bInter-American Commission on Human Rights  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured 2015-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe IACHR is a principal and autonomous organ of the Organization of American States (“OAS”) whose mission is to promote and protect human rights in the American hemisphere. It is composed of seven independent members who serve in a personal capacity. The website offers information about the Commission and its activities. Provides free online access to its annual report, reports on the situation of human rights in individual countries, and other special reports.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aHuman rightsxInternational cooperation. 0aHuman rights 0aInternational / multinational 5aAmericas 6aOrganization website2 aInter-American Commission on Human Rights,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n791332082 aOrganization of American States,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8012127840uhttp://www.oas.org/en/iachr/default.asp413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261101124428/http://www.oas.org/en/iachr/default.aspzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03072nai a22004097i wotm2015-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)91056212700aDeath penalty worldwide (Website).1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aDeath Penalty Worldwide 1aIthaca, N.Y :bCornell Law School  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured 2015-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aDeath Penalty Worldwide aims to bridge critical gaps in research and advocacy around the death penalty. The database on this Website provides information on the laws and practices related to the application of the death penalty for every country in the world that retains capital punishment and is designed to meet the needs of judges, policymakers, scholars, lawyers, journalists, and human rights advocates for comprehensive information of the death penalty. Also, it engages in targeted advocacy focusing on protecting the rights of those who come into conflict with the law, including juveniles, women, and individuals with intellectual disabilities and mental illnesses. Death Penalty Worldwide (DPW) was founded in April 2011 by Professor Sandra Babcock, Clinical Professor of Law and Director of the International Human Rights Clinic at Cornell University Law School, in partnership with the World Coalition Against the Death Penalty..cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCapital punishmentvDatabases. 0aHuman rightsvDatabases. 0aCivil rightsvDatabases. 0aCapital punishmentvStatistics. 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aHuman rights 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aStatistics.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261812 aCornell Law School,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8212722340uhttps://deathpenaltyworldwide.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250208121115/https://deathpenaltyworldwide.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02446nai a22004097i wotm2015-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuunyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)908954146  an-us-tx04aThe wrong Carlos.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aWrong Carlos 1aNew York :bCenter for Digital Research & Scholarship Columbia University Libraries/Information Services  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured 2015-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aOriginal site no longer available (checked 2026-11-10).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aSupplement to the book entitled “The Wrong Carlos.” This website provides extensive primary sources and videotaped witness interviews for the murder of a convenience store clerk named Wanda Vargas Lo´pez. It uncovers the controversial death penalty justice in Texas and is the case of Carlos DeLuna, who was executed in 1989.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aTrials (Murder)zTexas. 0aCapital punishmentzTexas. 0aJudicial errorzTexas. 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 5aTexas 6aDigital archive / collection 7aPrimary sources.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2022026079403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20231003182956/http://thewrongcarlos.netzArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.02220nai a22003497i wotm2015-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)236086005  an-us---00aCopyright.gov. 1aWashington, D.C :bU.S. Copyright Office  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured 2015-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Library of Congress U.S. Copyright Office is an office of public record for copyright registration and deposit of copyright materials. Copyright.gov is an official free public access website of the Library of Congress U.S. Copyright Office to provide thorough copyright information and its law. This website also includes the complete text of the Copyright Office’s Circulars, Brochures, and Factsheets.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCopyrightzUnited States. 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website2 aLibrary of Congress.bCopyright Office,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7911797140uhttps://copyright.gov/title17/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261023052213/https://copyright.gov/title17/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02419nai a22003977i wotm2015-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999vau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)904724961  an-us---00aNational Drug Court Resource Center. 1aAlexandria, VA :bNational Drug Court Institute  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured 2015-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe National Drug Court Resource Center is a virtual library of publications, factsheets, information on other drug courts, and sample forms. NDCRC includes information for both state and tribal drug court programs, as well as other restorative justice courts. NDCRC is hosted by the National Drug Court Institute, a professional services branch of the National Association of Drug Court Professionals.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aDrug courtszUnited StatesxComputer network resources. 0aDrug abusexTreatmentxLaw and legislationxComputer network resources. 0aSubstance abusexTreatmentxComputer network resources. 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 0aTribal / Indigenous nations 6aOrganization website40uhttps://ntcrc.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261026065059/https://ntcrc.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02898nai a22003737i wotm2015-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xx || d o    |0    2eng d  an-us---00aNational Inventory of Collateral Consequences of Conviction.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aNational Inventory of the Collateral Consequences of Conviction 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bThe Council of State Governments  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured 2015-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aPersons convicted of crime are subject to a wide variety of legal and regulatory sanctions and restrictions in addition to the sentence imposed by the court. These so-called “collateral consequences” of conviction have been promulgated with little coordination in disparate sections of state and federal codes, which makes it difficult for anyone to identify all of the penalties and disabilities that are triggered by conviction of a particular offense. Through the National Inventory, each jurisdiction’s collateral consequences will be made accessible to the public through a website that can be searched and sorted by categories and keywords.”–from the Web site.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aEx-convictsxLegal status, laws, etc.zUnited States. 0aCriminal recordszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008100369 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aStatutes and codes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110266112 aCouncil of State Governments,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7905495440uhttps://niccc.nationalreentryresourcecenter.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261022085543/https://niccc.nationalreentryresourcecenter.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03073nai a22003857i wotm2015-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999oku|| w o    |0    2eng d  an-us---00aIndian Affairs :bLaws and Treaties. 1a[Stillwater, Okla.] :bKappler, Charles J.  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured 2015-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aIndian Affairs: Laws and Treaties, compiled and edited by Charles J. Kappler, is an historically significant, seven volume compilation of U.S. treaties, laws and executive orders pertaining to Native American Indian tribes. The volumes cover U.S. Government treaties with Native Americans from 1778-1883 (Volume II) and U.S. laws and executive orders concerning Native Americans from 1871-1970 (Volumes I, III-VII). The work was first published in 1903-04 by the U.S. Government Printing Office. Enhanced by the editors’ use of margin notations and a comprehensive index, the information contained in Indian Affairs: Laws and Treaties is in high demand by Native peoples, researchers, journalists, attorneys, legislators, teachers and others of both Native and non-Native origins.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aIndians of North AmericaxTreaties.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85065399 0aIndians of North AmericaxLegal status, laws, etc.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85065315 0aIndians of North AmericaxGovernment relations.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85065278 0aIndigenous peoples & tribal law 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 0aTribal / Indigenous nations 7aTreaties.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026707 7aStatutes and codes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110266112 aOklahoma State University.bLibrary,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8005082440uhttps://dc.library.okstate.edu/digital/collection/kapplers413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20170111161934/http://digital.library.okstate.edu/kappler/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02945nai a22003737i wotm2015-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)897206035  acl-----04aThe Inter-American Court of Human Rights Project. 1aLos Angeles, California :bLoyola of Los Angeles International and Comparative Law Review  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured between 2014-10 and 2014-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aSearchable database which provides free public access to Inter-American Court decisions by case name, country, and topic. Included are a detailed case summary, case facts, procedural history, merits, and states compliance with the Inter-American Court’s judgment. The project produced by the editors and staff of the IACHR Project under the supervision of Professor Cesare Romano, allows users to search Inter-American Court decisions by case name, country, and topic. Advanced search features include the ability to search by specific violation of various Inter-American Conventions. When available, the database includes a link to a detailed case summary which includes case facts, procedural history, merits, and state compliance with the Inter-American Court’s judgment. To date, 74 detailed case summaries are available.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aInter-American Court of Human Rights.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n82045953 0aHuman rightszLatin America.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008122069 0aHuman rights 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102617240uhttps://iachr.lls.edu/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260714124850/https://iachr.lls.edu/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02688nai a22003857i wotm2015-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuunyu|| w o    |0    2eng d00aGuide to Foreign and International Legal Databases. 1a[New York] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured between 2014-09 and 2014-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aContinued by a successor resource (linked below).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, place of publication, 008 codes. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.  aThis guide is developed by the New York University Law Library and organizes database links under a number of broad categories. Included are databases for the European Union, the Council of Europe, environmental law, international criminal law, intellectual property, and human rights etc.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawxComputer network resources. 0aInternational lawxComputer network resources. 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aForeign & comparative law 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 6aResearch guide (web)2 aNew York University.bSchool of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8101338100tNYU Law Library: Foreign Law by Jurisdiction423Successor resource: NYU Law Library: Foreign Law by Jurisdictionuhttps://nyulaw.libguides.com/c.php?g=773850423Successor resource, archived version: NYU Law Library: Foreign Law by Jurisdictionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260302003107/https://nyulaw.libguides.com/c.php?g=773850zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.423Comparable current resource: GlobaLex: Other Global Research Toolsuhttps://www.law.nyu.edu/global/researchtools423Comparable current resource, archived version: GlobaLex: Other Global Research Toolsuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260731021923/https://www.law.nyu.edu/global/researchtoolszBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03534nai a22003977i wotm2015-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999at || d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)5087509800aWorld legal information institute :bWorldLII ; free, independent and non-profit access to worldwide law.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aWorld Legal Information Institute (Worldlii) 1aSydney :bAustralasian Legal Information Institute  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured between 2014-08 and 2014-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe World Legal Information Institute (WorldLII) is a collaborative legal information system, providing a key free source of primary legal materials (legislation and case law) for a comprehensive range of countries and jurisdictions worldwide. WorldLII has been developed by the Australasian Legal Information Institute and a number of partner institutions. The service combines access to all materials held in the AustLII, AsianLII, BAILII, CanLII, CommonLII, CyLaw, NZLII, PacLII, SAFLII and HKLII resource systems. The Global Legal Information Network (GLIN) of the United States Law Library of Congress is also searchable on this site. A database index enables users to browse information by country or region (Africa, Asia, Australasia, Europe, North America, Pacific islands) or by form of legal literature (legislation, case law, treaties, law reform, law journals and other materials). A sophisticated site search supported by online search tips and user guides is available. The World law Internet resource catalog gives links to non-WorldLII sources on the web. Other projects include a searchable database of decisions of all international and multi national courts and tribunals and a database containing all the academic law journals available via WorldLII.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aInternational lawxLegal research. 0aLawxComputer network resources. 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aForeign & comparative law 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261722 aAustralasian Legal Information Institute,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9602357440uhttp://www.worldlii.org413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261011002036/http://www.worldlii.orgzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02722nai a22003497i wotm2015-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)4282948400aOnline Ethics Center for Engineering and Science. 1aWashington, D.C :bNational Academy of Engineering  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2015).  aFeatured between 2014-07 and 2014-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aBased at the National Academy for Engineering, the Online Ethics Center aims to provide resources for understanding and addressing ethically significant problems that arise in science and engineering work, and to serve those who are promoting learning and advancing the understanding of responsible research and practice in science and engineering. The center is also intended to serve teachers of engineering and science students who want to include discussion of ethical problems closely related to technical subjects as part of science and engineering courses, or in free-standing subjects in professional and research ethics for students. The center’s website provides a wide range of materials including teaching and research ethics, safety and the environment, professional practice, computers and new technology, and a glossary.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aSciencexMoral and ethical aspects. 0aTechnologyxMoral and ethical aspects. 0aResearchxMoral and ethical aspects. 0aGeneral reference (not law-specific) 6aOrganization website2 aNational Academy of Engineering,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7911049040uhttps://onlineethics.virginia.edu/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20070121050958/http://onlineethics.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02702nai a22003617i wotm2016-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999inu|| w o    |0    2eng d  aa-ja---00aFloyd J. Mattice :bTokyo War Crimes Trial Scrapbook. 1a[Bloomington, Ind.] :bMattice, Floyd J.  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2016-08 and 2016-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aA digitized archival collection of personal and professional papers documenting Floyd Mattice’s time in Japan as the defense attorney for two of the accused Japanese war criminals. It is believed that the collection has been compiled by the secretary of Floyd Mattice. It eventually became the property of Mattice’s granddaughter Linda M. Prall of Bloomington, Indiana. Ms. Prall presented the scrapbook to the Jerome Hall Law Library in July, 2015. Included collections are official orders; memorandums; newspaper clippings; photographs; souvenirs; maps; telegraphs; typed manuscripts; a three page handwritten Japanese character manuscript; and handwritten personal letters to Mattice.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aTokyo Trial, Tokyo, Japan, 1946-1948.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85135870 0aWar crime trialszJapan.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85145165 0aInternational criminal law & transitional justice 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aInternational / multinational 5aJapan 6aDigital archive / collection 7aAlbums (Books).2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402617340uhttps://www.repository.law.indiana.edu/histdocs/9/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250831062813/https://www.repository.law.indiana.edu/histdocs/9/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02669nai a22003497i wotm2016-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xna|| d o    |0    2eng d00aLawCite. 1a[Sydney] :bAustralian Research Council  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2016-07 and 2016-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aLawCite is a free citation service for case law, giving reported and unreported citations and provides links to cases available in full on the world’s Legal Information Institutes. LawCite is useful in the context of a particular legal decision or law journal article. It will help you to locate and find a copy of a decision by citation, party names, jurisdiction, court, year or any combination of these. The system includes a relatively complete set of parallel citations and allows for things like alternative spelling of party names. It will also help you to see how a decision has been subsequently treated. The current emphasis is on common law countries, but this is being gradually extended to include civil law jurisdictions.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCitation of legal authoritiesvDatabases. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.vDatabases. 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aIndexes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261122 aAustralasian Legal Information Institute,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9602357440uhttps://www8.austlii.edu.au/LawCite/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260412223829/https://www8.austlii.edu.au/LawCite/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03018nai a22003857i wotm2016-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999at || d o    |0    2eng d00aCommonwealth Legal Information Institute :bFree access to Commonwealth and Common Law.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aCommonwealth Legal Information Institute 1a[Sydney] :bAustralasian Legal Information Institute  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2016-06 and 2016-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe main purpose of CommonLII is to provide a comparative law facility by which the laws of all common law countries can be searched and compared. In relation to case law, CommonLII assists in making the ideal of an international common law a reality, by providing the case law collection with the broadest geographical scope, and the only free access international citator (LawCite). As to legislation, CommonLII makes it easier to compare legislative developments in the same subject-areas across Commonwealth countries, and can assist in the process of law reform, as well as increasing the transparency of each country’s legal system. CommonLII also makes it easier to find commentaries on the law from Commonwealth and common law countries, including law reform reports and some law journals.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzCommonwealth countriesvDatabases. 0aCommon lawvDatabases. 0aComparative law.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85029299 0aForeign & comparative law 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261722 aAustralasian Legal Information Institute,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9602357440uhttp://www.commonlii.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261012031628/http://www.commonlii.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03082nai a22003857i wotm2016-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999mou|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)65199429  an-us---04aThe Federal Reserve Archival System for Economic Research (FRASER). 1a[St. Louis, Mo.] :bFederal Reserve Bank of St Louis  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2016-05 and 2016-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Federal Reserve Archival System for Economic Research (FRASER) started in 2004 as a data preservation and accessibility project of the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis. FRASER’s mission is to safeguard and provide easy access to economic history—particularly the history of the Federal Reserve System. FRASER consists of an open archive of economic statistical publications and data within an automated system to retrieve both images and data. There are two kinds of documents on FRASER: 1) data publications from U.S. government agencies such as the Bureau of Labor Statistics and the Census Bureau, and others more specialized, like the Board of Governor’s Statistical Releases; and, 2) historical publications such as archival material from economic policymakers, and special collections: source materials from Allan Meltzer’s A History of the Federal Reserve.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aBoard of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (U.S.)0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n78091283 0aBanks and bankingzUnited StatesxHistoryvSources. 0aMonetary policyzUnited StatesxHistory.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2010102268 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection 7aStatistics.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261812 aFederal Reserve Bank of St. Louis,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n5000115340uhttps://fraser.stlouisfed.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261017163921/https://fraser.stlouisfed.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03577nai a22004337i wotm2016-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuubn || w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)946642260  ae-bn---00aHuman Rights Chamber for Bosnia and Herzegovina = Dom za ljudsk prava za Bosnu i Hercegovinu.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aHuman Rights Chamber for Bosnia and Herzegovina 1aMusala, Sarajevo :bHuman Rights Chamber for Bosnia and Herzegovina  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2016-04 and 2016-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aOriginal site no longer available (checked 2026-11-10).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Human Rights Chamber is a judicial body established under Annex 6 to the General Framework Agreement for Peace in Bosnia and Herzegovina (Dayton Peace Agreement) The Chamber has the order to consider alleged or apparent violations of human rights as provided in the European Convention for the Protection of Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms. Particular priority is given to allegations of especially severe or systematic violations, as well as those founded on alleged discrimination on prohibited grounds. The Chamber is composed of 14 members. Four members were appointed by the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina and two by the Republika Srpska . The remaining eight members are internationals and were appointed by the Committee of Ministers of the Council of Europe. The members appointed are all distinguished lawyers and bring to the Chamber a wide variety of experience in different backgrounds including the judiciary, the academic sphere, private legal practice, administration and politics, and international, criminal and human rights law.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aHuman rightszBosnia and HerzegovinavCases. 0aCrimes against humanityzBosnia and HerzegovinavCases. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.zBosnia and Herzegovina. 0aHuman rights 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aInternational criminal law & transitional justice 0aForeign (single country or region) 0aInternational / multinational 5aBosnia and Herzegovina 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261722 aHuman Rights Chamber for Bosnia and Herzegovina,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no2003044955403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20210121214450/http://www.hrc.ba/ENGLISH/DEFAULT.HTMzArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.02578nai a22003737i wotm2016-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999vau|| w o    s0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)891187587  an-us-va00aWythepedia :bthe George Wythe encyclopedia. 1aWilliamsburg, Virginia :bWolf Law Library, William & Mary Law School  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2016-03 and 2016-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aGeorge Wythe is preeminent in the list of Virginia’s revolutionary founding fathers, and his life and careers as a teacher and judge gave him the opportunity to have a profound impact on the history of the Commonwealth and the United States. George Wythe is a signatory of the Declaration of Independence and the first law professor in America, and chancery court judge. Wythe was born in Elizabeth City County, Virginia and spent the majority of his life in the Commonwealth, only traveling outside it to attend the Second Continental Congress and the Constitutional Convention in Philadelphia.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aJudgeszVirginiavCorrespondence. 0aUnited StatesxHistoryy1783-1815vSources. 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 5aVirginia 7aEncyclopedias.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402609240uhttps://wythepedia.wm.edu/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250818220605/https://lawlibrary.wm.edu/wythepedia/index.php/Main_PagezBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02938nai a22004097i wotm2016-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999lu || w o    i0    2mul d  a(OCoLC)406010757  ae------00aCuria (Court of Justice of the European Union).1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aCuria 1aLuxembourg, Luxembourg :bCourt of Justice of the European Union  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2016-02 and 2016-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aOfficial website of the Court of Justice of the European Union and its constituent courts. The site provides access to information about the Court of Justice, the General Court (formerly the Court of First Instance of the European Communities), and the Civil Service Tribunal. It includes the full text of judgments, orders, opinions and notices, and allows searching of cases from the courts. The specific mission of CJEU is to ensure that the law is observed in the interpretation and application of the Treaties of the European Union by 1) reviewing the legality of actions taken by the EU’s institutions; 2) enforcing compliance by member states with their obligations under the Treaties, and 3) interpreting European Union law.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.zEuropean Union countries. 0aCourtszEuropean Union countries. 0aEuropezEuropean Union countries. 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aEuropean Union 6aGovernment agency website 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261722 aCourt of Justice of the European Union,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201119564440uhttps://curia.europa.eu/site/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261109080244/https://curia.europa.eu/site/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02926nai a22003857i wotm2016-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)53897049  an-us---00aLegal Information Institute. 1aIthaca, NY :bLegal Information Institute, Cornell Law School  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2016-01 and 2016-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe LII is known internationally as a leading “law-not-com” provider of public legal information and offers all opinions of the United States Supreme Court handed down since 1992, together with over 600 earlier decisions selected for their historic importance, over a decade of opinions of the New York Court of Appeals, and the full United States Code. The LII also publishes important secondary sources: libraries in two important areas (legal ethics and social security) and a series of “topical” pages that serve as concise explanatory guides and Internet resource listings for roughly 100 areas of law. The LII of world legal materials gathers the Internet-accessible sources of the constitutions, statutes, judicial opinions, and it also holds resources and document collections of International law. Search engines and ranking systems identify the LII as the most linked-to web resource in the field of law.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzUnited States. 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aInternational law & organizations 0aForeign & comparative law 0aUnited States (federal) 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102617240uhttps://www.law.cornell.edu/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261106024939/https://www.law.cornell.edu/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02837nai a22003377i wotm2016-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999enk|| d o    |0    2eng d00aFLARE Index to Treaties. 1a[London] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2015-12 and 2016-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe FLARE Index to Treaties (FIT) was launched in March 2009 on the Institute of Advanced Legal Studies web server. The Index has established itself as a valuable finding tool for the international lawyer. It is a fully searchable database indexing and listing over 2,000 of the most significant multilateral treaties concluded from 1353 onwards and a number of significant bilateral treaties signed between1353 and 1815. The Index was based on two groups of material: - Just over 1,000 or so entries relating to significant multilateral treaties concluded between 1856 and 1994 contained in the print publication: Multilateral Treaties: index and current status,compiled and annotated within the University of Nottingham Treaty Center by M.J. Bowman and D.J. Harris (London: Butterworths, 1984, tenth supplement, 1994) – hereafter referred to as Bowman & Harris. - About 500 treaties either concluded after the final edition of Bowman & Harris was published or which were only footnoted in the publication.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aTreatiesvIndexes. 0aInternational lawvIndexes. 0aInternational law & organizations 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aIndexes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402611240uhttps://libguides.ials.sas.ac.uk/az/flare-index-to-treaties413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20241130101315/https://libguides.ials.sas.ac.uk/az/flare-index-to-treatieszBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03229nai a22004457i wotm2016-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)912968253  an-us---an-cn---00aIndigenous law portal. 1aWashington, D.C :bLibrary of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2015-11 and 2016-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aOur highly regarded colleague, Dr. Jolande E. Goldberg, and her cohorts at the Library of Congress have successfully mapped constitutions,legal materials, and legislative documents of American Indians as well as Native Hawaiians on the Indigenous Law Portal website. This rich portal website provides a wide array of descriptions of indigenous peoples of the Americas and connects their cultures, history, and lives to the indigenous law classification. The portal would also serve as an extensive study-aid for tribal studies of Americans. We owe Dr. Jolande E.Goldberg, staff at the Library of Congress, and related constituencies a debt of gratitude for their hard work and collaboration to make this great portal website available.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aConstitutionszUnited States. 0aConstitutionszCanada. 0aConstitutionszMexico. 0aIndians of South AmericaxPolitics and government. 0aIndians of South AmericaxLegal status, laws, etc. 0aIndians of North AmericaxLegal status, laws, etc. 0aIndians of North AmericaxPolitics and government. 0aIndigenous peoples & tribal law 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aTribal / Indigenous nations 6aPortal or gateway (links to other sites) 7aConstitutions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261672 aLaw Library of Congress (U.S.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n9104313740uhttps://www.loc.gov/research-centers/law-library-of-congress/collections/digital-collections/indigenous-law/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260211134734/https://www.loc.gov/research-centers/law-library-of-congress/collections/digital-collections/indigenous-law/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02762nai a22003977i wotm2016-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)56906688  an-us---00aUnited States courts (Website).1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aUnited States Courts 1aWashington, D.C :bAdministrative Office of the United States Courts  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2015-10 and 2016-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aProvides access to the federal rules and forms in effect, information on the rulemaking process, including proposed and pending rules amendments, and historical and archival records. The annual report of the Director addresses the workload of all federal courts, the federal probation and pretrial services system. Federal court management statistics give profiles for regional courts of appeals and district courts. In the video series, Pathways to the Bench, individual judges talk about the personal, character-building challenges in their lives that prepared them to serve on the bench. Each judge has a motivational message for others.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCourtszUnited States. 0aJudicial statisticszUnited States. 0aJudgeszUnited States. 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website 7aStatistics.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261811 aUnited States.bAdministrative Office of the United States Courts,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8101408040uhttps://www.uscourts.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261110080404/https://www.uscourts.gov/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02383nai a22003977i wotm2016-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999mdu|| d o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)314427746  an-us---04aThe National Archives. 1aCollege Park, MD :bNational Archives and Records Administration  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2016).  aFeatured between 2015-09 and 2015-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  a“The National Archives and Records Administration (NARA) is the nation’s record keeper. Of all documents and materials created in the course of business conducted by the United States Federal government.” –from the Web site.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aArchiveszUnited StatesvDatabases. 0aElectronic public recordszUnited States. 0aElectronic government informationzUnited States. 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260811 aUnited States.bNational Archives and Records Administration,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8417610140uhttps://www.archives.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261110080342/https://www.archives.gov/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02837nai a22003977i wotm2017-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1020497435  an-us---00aCollection, federal courts web archive.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aFederal Courts Web Archive 1aWashington, D.C :bLibrary of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured between 2017-10 and 2017-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Federal Courts Web Archive provides unique resources for scholars and others conducting retrospective research into the work of the federal judiciary.These sites contain a wide variety of resources prepared by federal courts, such as: slip opinions, transcripts, dockets, court rules, calendars, announcements, judicial biographies, statistics, educational resources, and reference materials. The materials available on the federal court websites were created to support a diverse array of users and needs, including attorneys and their clients, pro se litigants seeking to represent themselves, jurors, visitors to the court, and community outreach programs.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCourtszUnited StatesxHistoryvSources. 0aLawzUnited StatesvCases. 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261722 aLibrary of Congress,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7808903540uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/federal-courts-web-archive/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260211112157/https://www.loc.gov/collections/federal-courts-web-archive/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02606nai a22004097i wotm2017-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999tnu|| w o    s0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1004308041  an-us---04aThe First Amendment encyclopedia :barticles, news and insights about free expression in America.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aThe First Amendment Encyclopedia 1aMurfreesboro, TN :bMiddle Tennessee State University  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured 2017-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aMiddle Tennessee State University has launched a searchable website with more than thousands of essays and articles about court decisions and doctrines; people, law and events; and general issues and organizations significant in the First Amendment’s history in the United States.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.10aUnited States.tConstitution.n1st AmendmentvEncyclopedias.07aConstitution (United States)2fast0(OCoLC)fst01356075 0aFreedom of expressionzUnited StatesvEncyclopedias. 7aFreedom of expression.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01200263 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 7aEncyclopedias.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260922 aMiddle Tennessee State University,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n5007167640uhttps://firstamendment.mtsu.edu/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261012073455/https://firstamendment.mtsu.edu/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03827nai a22004937i wotm2017-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1012404142  an-us---an-us-tn00aJames K. Polk papers. 1aWashington, DC :bLibrary of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured 2017-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe papers of James K. Polk (1795-1849), governor of Tennessee, representative from Tennessee, Speaker of the House of Representatives, and eleventh president of the United States, contain approximately 20,500 items dating from 1775 to 1891, with the bulk falling in the period 1830-1849. The collection includes correspondence, presidential letterbooks, diaries, speeches and messages, account and memorandum books, family papers, financial and legal records, printed matter, portraits, and other papers relating chiefly to Polk’s political career in Tennessee and on the national level. The Library of Congress acquired approximately 10,000 items from Mrs. Fall in 1903. The Chicago Historical Society transferred additional correspondence and the bulk of Polk’s diaries to the Library of Congress in 1910. Sadie Fall Gardner Grant (Mrs. Rollin P. Grant), a daughter of Sarah Polk’s niece, augmented the Polk papers with a significant donation of materials in 1927. Between the years 1954 and 1973, the Library of Congress produced a microfilm edition of the materials in series 1-9 and 11, with a related index published in 1969. In the years since, scattered extant original documents have been added to the collection.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.10aPolk, James K.q(James Knox),d1795-1849vArchives.17aPolk, James K.q(James Knox),d1795-1849.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00063709 0aPresidentszUnited StatesvArchives. 0aGovernorszTennesseevArchives. 7aPresidents.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01075723 7aGovernors.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00945704 7aPolitics and government.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01919741 0aUnited StatesxPolitics and governmenty1845-1849vSources. 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 7aTennessee.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01205353 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 5aTennessee 6aDigital archive / collection 7aPrimary sources.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20220260792 aLibrary of Congress,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7808903540uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/james-k-polk-papers/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260823164141/https://www.loc.gov/collections/james-k-polk-papers/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02795nai a22004217i wotm2017-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)439754867  an-us---00aTTB :bAlcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau. U.S. Department of the Treasury.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aAlcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau (TTB) 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bUnited States Department of the Treasury  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured 2017-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Alcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau (TTB) protects the public by enforcing the provisions of the Federal Alcohol Administration Act (FAA Act) to ensure that only qualified persons engage in the alcohol beverage industry. TTB is responsible for enforcing the laws regulating alcohol production, importation, and wholesale businesses; tobacco manufacturing and importing businesses; and alcohol labeling and advertising.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.10aUnited States.bAlcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau.17aUnited States.bAlcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01917663 0aAlcoholxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aTobaccoxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 7aAlcoholxLaw and legislation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00804272 7aTobaccoxLaw and legislation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01151831 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website1 aUnited States.bAlcohol and Tobacco Tax and Trade Bureau,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200511175640uhttps://www.ttb.gov/index.shtml413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20170126001805/https://www.ttb.gov/index.shtmlzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02971nai a22004577i wotm2017-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xx || w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)100056840400aCISG Advisory Council. 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bThe United Nations Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods (CISG) Council Advisory  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured 2017-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe United Nations Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods (CISG) Council Advisory has three main functions: to promote understanding and uniform interpretation of the CISG by publishing opinions on issues of interpretation, to promote the CISG generally, and to encourage and assist with the adoption and implementation of the CISG in jurisdictions that have not ratified it. The Advisory Council’s principal role is promoting uniform interpretation of the CISG.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aCISG Advisory Council.00aUnited Nations Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goodsd(1980 April 11)vCases.07aUnited Nations Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods (1980 April 11)2fast0(OCoLC)fst01773899 0aExport sales contractsvCases. 0aForeign trade regulationvCases. 0aConflict of lawsxSalesvCases. 0aLawxInternational unificationvCases. 7aExport sales contracts.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00918736 7aForeign trade regulation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00931923 7aConflict of lawsxSales.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00874989 7aLawxInternational unification.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00993755 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 6aOrganization website2 aCISG Advisory Council,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201708585540uhttps://cisgac.com/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261012061729/https://cisgac.com/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02784nai a22003737i wotm2017-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuuvau|| w o    |0    2eng d00aLawArXiv :bAn open access community for legal scholarship.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aLawArXiv 1a[Charlottesville, Virginia] :bCenter for Open Science  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured between 2017-03 and 2017-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aContinued by a successor resource (linked below).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe LawArXiv repository was developed jointly by the Legal Information Preservation Alliance, the Mid-American Law Library Consortium, NELLCO, and the Cornell Law Library, with the Center for Open Science (COS) providing the technological infrastructure via its Open Source Framework. The COS platform also serves as a preprint service, allowing organizations to control their branding, licensing requirements and taxonomy. LawArXiv will accept preprints and post prints where the author has the copyright on their work.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aPreprints.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85106389 0aOpen access publishing.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2005002533 0aLawxElectronic information resources. 0aLegal scholarship & law journals 6aDigital archive / collection2 aCenter for Open Science,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n20151800532 aLegal Information Preservation Alliance,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201915852200tLawArXiv on OSF Preprints423Successor resource: LawArXiv on OSF Preprintsuhttps://osf.io/preprints/lawarxiv/discover423Successor resource, archived version: LawArXiv on OSF Preprintsuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20251209203035/https://osf.io/preprints/lawarxiv/discoverzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02995nai a22003857i wotm2017-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  ae-uk---ae------00aBrexit. 1a[San Francisco, California] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured between 2017-02 and 2017-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aBrexit is a commonly used term for the United Kingdom’s planned withdrawal from the European Union. Brexit is an abbreviation for “British exit,” referring to the 2016 referendum vote to leave the UK by April 2019. Issues in the United Kingdom European membership referendum, 2016 are the economic, human and political issues that were discussed during the campaign about the withdrawal of the UK from the EU. The leave campaign argues that a reduction in red tape associated with EU regulations would create more jobs and that small to medium sized companies who trade domestically would be the biggest beneficiaries. In just 264 words in five paragraphs, article 50 of the Lisbon treaty sets out how an EU member can voluntarily leave the European Union. It specifies that a leaver should notify the European council of its intention, negotiate a deal on its withdrawal and establish legal grounds for a future relationship with the EU.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aEuropean UnionzGreat Britain.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2017003454 0aGreat BritainxForeign relationszEuropean Union countries. 0aEuropean Union countriesxForeign relationszGreat Britain. 0aForeign & comparative law 0aInternational law & organizations 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aUnited Kingdom 5aEuropean Union 6aResearch guide (web)2 aWikimedia Foundation,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n200507027240uhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brexit413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261101211205/https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BrexitzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03051nai a22003737i wotm2017-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| d o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)5066636800aUnited Nations treaty collection = Nations Unies collection des traités.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aUnited Nations Treaty Collection 1aNew York, N.Y :bUnited Nations  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured between 2017-01 and 2017-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe United Nations Treaty Series (UNTS) is the result of article 102 of the Charter of the United Nations, which states as below. A treaty series includes status and full text of all multilateral treaties deposited with the United Nations. The UNTS is by far the largest collection f treaties, running some 2800 volumes containing 158,00 treaties from 1946. Its predecessor was the League of Nations Treaty Series (LNTS). Charter of the United Nations: 1. Every treaty and every international agreement entered into by any Member of the United Nations after the present Charter comes into force shall as soon as possible be registered with the Secretariat and published by it. 2. No party to any such treaty or international agreement which has not been registered in accordance with the provisions of paragraph 1 of this Article may invoke that treaty or agreement before any organ of the United Nations.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aUnited Nations.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n79021345 0aTreatiesvDatabases. 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aTreaties.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110267072 aUnited Nations,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7902134540uhttps://treaties.un.org/Pages/Home.aspx?lang=en413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261011035012/https://treaties.un.org/Pages/Home.aspx?lang=enzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03201nai a22004097i wotm2017-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999mdu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)613044156  an-us---00aNational Institute on Drug Abuse :badvancing addiction science.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aNational Institute on Drug Abuse 1aBethesda, MD :bU.S. Department of Health and Human Services, National Institutes of Health, National Institute on Drug Abuse  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured between 2016-12 and 2017-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe National Institute on Drug Abuse (NIDA) is a federal-government research institute in the U.S. whose mission is to “lead the Nation in bringing the power of science to bear on drug abuse and addiction.” The mission of the National Institute on Drug Abuse (NIDA) is to advance science on the causes and consequences of drug use and addiction and to apply that knowledge to improve individual and public health. In this regard, NIDA addresses the most fundamental and essential questions about drug abuse — from detecting and responding to emerging drug abuse trends and understanding how drugs work in the brain and body, to developing and testing new approaches to treatment and prevention. The institute has conducted an in-depth study of addiction according to its biological, behavioral and social components. It has also supported many treatments such as nicotine patches and gums, and performed research into AIDS and other drug-related diseases.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aDrug abusexPreventionzUnited States. 0aDrug addictionxTreatmentxResearchzUnited States. 0aDrug abusezUnited StatesxPrevention. 0aDrug abusexPrevention. 0aDrug addictionxTreatment. 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website2 aNational Institute on Drug Abuse,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7909674440uhttp://www.drugabuse.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250220093439/http://www.drugabuse.gov/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02715nai a22003737i wotm2017-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)458023518  an-us---00aAmerican Immigration Lawyers Association. 1aWashington, DC :bAmerican Immigration Lawyers Association  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured between 2016-11 and 2017-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe American Immigration Lawyers Association (AILA) is the national association of immigration lawyers and law professors who practice and teach immigration law. Established in 1946, AILA is a nonpartisan, not-for-profit organization that provides continuing legal education, information, professional services, and expertise to advance the quality of immigration and nationality law and practice, and enhance the professional development of its members. There is a full text resource guide examining the issues around immigration law reform. The site also includes a searchable directory of immigration lawyers and a page of related web links. Parts of the site are restricted to members only.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aEmigration and immigration lawzUnited States. 0aImmigration & citizenship 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aUnited States (federal) 6aOrganization website 7aDirectories.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260872 aAmerican Immigration Lawyers Association,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8682056740uhttps://www.aila.org:443/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261030073503/https://www.aila.org:443/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02849nai a22003737i wotm2017-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuudcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)756838039  an-us---00aDisability.gov. 1aWashington, DC :bU.S. Department of Labor's Office of Disability Employment Policy  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured between 2016-10 and 2017-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aNo verified current address for the original site could be found.  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aA website of the Disability.gov provides access to the federal government comprehensive information on disability-related programs, services, policies, laws and regulations. There are links to thousands of resources from many different federal government agencies, as well as state and local governments and nonprofit organizations across the country. Ten main subject areas: Benefits, Civil Rights, Community Life, Education, Emergency Preparedness, Employment, Health, Housing, Technology and Transportation are updated daily. Disability.gov is also an information and referral website to direct you to another website pertaining to your research topic. You can learn more about how to find, connect, and share on the site’s fact sheet which is available in the Newsroom.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aPeople with disabilitiesxGovernment policyzUnited States. 0aPeople with disabilitiesxGovernment policy. 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website 6aPortal or gateway (links to other sites)1 aUnited States.bOffice of Disability Employment Policy,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no2001102384403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20160104144821/http://www.disability.gov/zArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.02233nai a22003017i 4500001001200000005001700012006001900029007001500048008004100063245004100104264007200145300002200217336002600239337002600265338003600291500008800327500008100415500007000496500018000566520058300746588012001329650002001449650008501469653003701554653003301591856009601624856021101720wotm2017-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuuxx || w o    |0    2eng d00aNELLCO Legal Scholarship Repository. 1a[Place of publication not identified] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2017).  aFeatured between 2016-09 and 2017-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aNo verified current address for the original site could be found.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, 008 codes. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.  aNELLCO’s legal scholarship repository brings Institutional Repositories (IRs) together all of a University’s research under one umbrella and is a service of the NELLCO libraries. Presentations, senior theses, and other works not published elsewhere can be also published in the NELLCO IR. The website is a vehicle for working papers or copies of published articles and conference papers. Research and scholarly output included in this website has been selected and deposited by the individual university departments and centers on campuscLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawvDatabases. 0aInstitutional repositories.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2006003967 0aLegal scholarship & law journals 6aDigital archive / collection423Comparable current resource: Digital Commons Network: Lawuhttps://network.bepress.com/law/423Comparable current resource, archived version: Digital Commons Network: Lawuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261025121108/https://network.bepress.com/law/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02979nai a22003497i wotm2018-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  an-us---00aUnited States Office of Government Ethics. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bUnited States Office of Government Ethics  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2018-09 and 2019-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aUnder the Ethics in Government Act of 1978 the United States Office of Government Ethics (OGE) was created and became an independent agency in 1989 to prevent and resolve conflict of interest. OGE oversees and provides overall leadership to the executive branch ethics program and works with a community of ethics practitioners made up of nearly 5,000 ethics officials in more than 130 agencies to implement that program. OGE has also produced substantive analyses and provided testimony on executive branch ethics laws at the request of Congress. In addition, OGE regularly provides information to the Government Accountability Office as it prepares ethics-related reports. One of OGE’s missions is to foster high ethical standards for executive branch employees and to strengthen the public’s confidence that the Government’s business is conducted with impartiality and integrity involves regular communication with the Congress.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aUnited States. Office of Government Ethics.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n80039458 0aConflict of interestszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008101072 0aCivil service ethicszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008101071 0aElections, ethics & government accountability 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website1 aUnited States.bOffice of Government Ethics,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8003945840uhttps://www.oge.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261017105436/https://www.oge.gov/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02963nai a22004097i wotm2018-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)44305752  an-us---00aGSA :bU.S. General Services Administration.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aGSA (General Services Administration) 1aWashington, DC :bGeneral Services Administration  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2018-08 and 2018-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe U.S. General Services Administration (GSA) established in 1949, is an independent agency to help mange and support the basic function of federal agencies. GSA also supplies products and communications for U.S. government offices, provides transportation and office space to federal employees, and develops government-wide cost-minimizing policies and other management tasks. GSA’s business lines include the Federal Acquisition Service (FAS) and the Public Buildings Service (PBS), as well as several Staff Offices including the Office of Government-wide Policy, the Office of Small Business Utilization, and the Office of Mission Assurance. As part of FAS, GSA’s Technology Transformation Services (TTS) helps federal agencies improve delivery of information and services to the public.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aGovernment purchasingzUnited States. 0aGovernment propertyzUnited States. 0aReal estate managementzUnited States. 0aPublic buildingszUnited StatesxManagement. 0aPublic buildingsxManagement. 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website1 aUnited States.bGeneral Services Administration,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7903481840uhttps://www.gsa.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261109042132/https://www.gsa.gov/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02786nai a22004097i wotm2018-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)51561809  an-us---00aRegulations.gov :byour voice in Federal decision-making.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aregulations.gov 1aWashington, D.C :bEnvironmental Protection Agency  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2018-07 and 2018-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aRegulations.gov is a U. S. Federal government Website that allows users to make public comments in response to notices of proposed rulemaking issued by participating agencies; such comments become part of the public record and may be displayed on the site. From the Regulations.gov, you can view a description of every proposed and final Federal regulation currently open for comment, read the full text of the regulations, and submit your comments to the Federal agencies responsible for the rule making action. Searches by government agency name or by keyword are availablecLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aDelegated legislationzUnited States. 0aLegislationzUnited States. 0aAdministrative lawzUnited States. 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website 7aAdministrative regulations.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110260301 aUnited States.bEnvironmental Protection Agency,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7907959740uhttps://www.regulations.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261017125326/http://www.regulations.govzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03498nai a22004337i wotm2018-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)44350120  an-us---00aChildren's Bureau. 1aWashington, D.C :bU.S. Department of Health and Human Services Administration for Children and Families  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2018-06 and 2018-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Children’s Bureau (CB) is the first federal agency within the U.S. Government-and in fact, the world. The CB partners with federal, state, tribal and local agencies to improve the overall health and well-being of our nation’s children and families. Within an annual budge, the bureau provides support and guidance to programs that focus on: - Strengthening families and preventing child abuse and neglect - Protecting children when abuse or neglect has occurred - Ensuring that every child and youth has a permanent family or family connection The Bureau also participates in a variety of projects, including: - Providing guidance on federal law, policy and program regulations - Funding essential services, helping states and tribes operate every aspect of their child welfare systems - Supporting innovation through competitive, peer-reviewed grants for research and program development - Offering training and technical assistance to improve child welfare service delivery - Monitoring child welfare services to help states and tribes achieve positive outcomes for children and families - Sharing research to help child welfare professionals improve their servicescLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aChild welfarexGovernment policyzUnited States. 0aChild abusezUnited StatesxPrevention. 0aFoster home carezUnited States. 0aAdoptionzUnited States. 0aChild abusexPrevention. 0aChild welfarexGovernment policy. 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website1 aUnited States.bChildren's Bureau,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n800447451 aUnited States.bAdministration for Children and Families,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9300028240uhttps://acf.gov:443/cb413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250205153233/https://www.acf.hhs.gov/cbzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02829nai a22003377i wotm2018-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999au || w o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)4435603600aUnited Nations Office for Outer Space Affairs. 1aVienna, Austria :bOffice for Outer Space Affairs, Unite Nations Office at Vienna  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2018-05 and 2018-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe United Nations Office for Outer Space Affairs (UNOOSA) is the United Nations office responsible for promoting international cooperation in the peaceful uses of outer space. UNOOSA serves as the secretariat for the General Assembly’s only committee dealing exclusively with international cooperation in the peaceful uses of outer space: the United Nations Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space (COPUOS). UNOOSA is also responsible for implementing the Secretary-General’s responsibilities under international space law and maintaining the United Nations Register of Objects Launched into Outer Space. The Committee on the Peaceful Uses of Outer Space is the forum for the development of international space law. Each of the treaties stresses the notion that outer space, the activities carried out in outer space to enhance the well-being of all countries and humankind, with an emphasis on promoting international cooperation.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aAstronauticsxInternational cooperation. 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 6aOrganization website2 aUnited Nations.bOffice for Outer Space Affairs,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n9303009040uhttps://www.unoosa.org/oosa/index.html413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261017150232/https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/index.htmlzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02924nai a22004217i wotm2018-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999mau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1035559132  an-us---00aGavel-to-Gavel :bthe Watergate Scandal and Public Television. 1aCulpeper, Virginia :bAmerican Archive of Public Broadcasting  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2018-04 and 2018-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe American Archive of Public Broadcasting recently published an online exhibit called “Gavel to Gavel: The Watergate Scandal and Public Television, which includes newly digitized material from the Senate Watergate hearings as well as coverage of the 1974 House impeachment hearings, The exhibit provides access to context for the hearings not only for public broadcasting, but also for America as a whole and covers all the coverage, a highlights reel, an episode guide and an essay putting the coverage into historical perspective.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aBroadcasting archiveszUnited States. 0aPublic televisionzUnited States. 0aAudio-visual archiveszUnited States. 0aUnited StatesxPolitics and governmenty1969-1974vSources. 0aElections, ethics & government accountability 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection 7aPrimary sources.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20220260792 aAmerican Archive of Public Broadcasting,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n20150364792 aWGBH Educational Foundation,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n5005271340uhttps://americanarchive.org/exhibits/watergate413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260422202318/https://americanarchive.org/exhibits/watergatezBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02719nai a22003977i wotm2018-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999oku|| d o    |0    2eng d  an-us---an-us-ak00aAmerican Indian and Alaskan Native Documents in the Congressional Serial Set, 1817-1899. 1a[Norman, Oklahoma] :bNicely, Marilyn K.  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2018-03 and 2018-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe database provides free, online access to historic full-text government documents related to American Indian and Alaskan Natives for the benefit of tribes, scholars, students and the public in general. The Serial Set includes House and Senate Documents and Reports from Congressional committees dealing with proposed legislation and issues under investigation, as well as materials from the executive branch.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aIndians of North AmericaxGovernment relationsvSources. 0aIndians of North AmericaxLegal status, laws, etc.vSources. 0aAlaska NativesxLegal status, laws, etc.vSources. 0aIndigenous peoples & tribal law 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (federal) 0aTribal / Indigenous nations 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aLegislative materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263622 aUniversity of Oklahoma.bCollege of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7907877040uhttps://digitalcommons.law.ou.edu/indianserialset/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261001014306/https://digitalcommons.law.ou.edu/indianserialset/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02124nai a22003497i wotm2018-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)90405645400aCenter for Art Law :bart and cultural heritage law aggregator.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aCenter for Art Law 1aBrooklyn, NY :bCenter for Art Law  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2018-02 and 2018-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Center for Art Law Website provides access to a coherent Art Law community and a centralized resource for Art and Cultural Heritage Law. It strives to connect artists, scholars, attorneys, students and related professionals through listing online resources, publications and organizations dealing with art law, legal and non-legal.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLaw and artvCases. 0aArt & cultural heritage 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 6aOrganization website 6aPortal or gateway (links to other sites)40uhttps://itsartlaw.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261012122712/https://itsartlaw.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02668nai a22003857i wotm2018-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1028580275  an-us---00aCollection U. S. Code.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aThe United States Code 1aWashington, D.C :bLibrary of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2018-01 and 2018-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe United States Code– is a compilation of federal statutory law arranged by subject by the House Office of Law Revision Council. Each title in the United States Code corresponds to a subject. For example, title 18 concerns crimes and criminal procedure. 18 U.S.C. § 1 is an example a citation in the Code. 18 indicates the title, U.S.C. is an abbreviation for the United States Code, and 1 is the section number of a particular statute within title 18.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85075193 0aLegislationzUnited States. 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (federal) 7aStatutes and codes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110266112 aLibrary of Congress,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7808903540uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/united-states-code/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260820155826/https://www.loc.gov/collections/united-states-code/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.423Comparable current resource: United States Code (Office of the Law Revision Counsel)uhttps://uscode.house.gov/03174nai a22004217i wotm2018-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1002309156  an-us---00aAlexander Hamilton papers. 1aWashington, DC :bLibrary of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured between 2017-12 and 2018-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Library holds the world’s largest collection of Hamilton papers-approximately 12,000 items concentrated from 1777 until Hamilton’s death in 1804, including letters, legal papers and drafts of speeches and writings, among other items. Now, for the first time, these original documents-many in Hamilton’s own hand-will be available for researchers, students or the generally curious anywhere in the world to explore on the Website. Items in the collection include: - A letter written when Hamilton was 12 or 13 to his friend Edward Stevens describing his wish to raise his station in life; - The outline of Hamilton’s speech at the Constitutional Convention; - Hamilton’s draft of George Washington’s farewell address; - His draft of the infamous Reynolds pamphlet; - A letter to his wife, Eliza, written shortly before his fatal duel with Aaron Burr.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aUnited States Department of the TreasuryvArchives. 0aStatesmenzUnited StatesvArchives. 0aNew York (State)xPolitics and governmenty1775-1865vSources. 0aUnited StatesxPolitics and governmenty1783-1809vSources. 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 5aNew York 6aDigital archive / collection 7aPrimary sources.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20220260792 aLibrary of Congress.bManuscript Division,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7808064240uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/alexander-hamilton-papers/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260814161832/https://www.loc.gov/collections/alexander-hamilton-papers/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03403nai a22004697i wotm2018-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuuazu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1007852090  an-us---00aKey public record resources for legal and investigative professionals.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aPublic Record Authority 1a[Tempe, Arizona] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2018).  aFeatured 2017-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aOriginal site no longer available (checked 2026-11-10).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aBRB Publications, is the nation’s premier publisher of reference sources and websites used for public records. BRB Publications is a past recipient of the Publisher of the Year award from Quality Books and is a past winner of a Product Achievement Award from the Information Industry Association. BRB’s data goes well beyond a Google Search. The metadata provided by BRB includes in-depth descriptions of record access policies and procedures, access methods, restrictions, fees, turn around times, identifiers shown on records and other needed tips and information used for public record searching.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger.20aPublic Record Retriever NetworkvDirectories. 0aPublic recordszUnited States. 0aGovernment informationzUnited States. 0aElectronic public recordszUnited States. 7aPublic recordsxLaw and legislation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01082870 7aGovernment information.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00945364 7aElectronic public records.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00907430 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aDirectories.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402608700tBRB Public Record Research System403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20160114090530/https://www.brbpublications.com/Default.aspxzArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.423Successor resource: BRB Public Record Research Systemuhttps://brbpublications.com/products/public-record-research-system423Successor resource, archived version: BRB Public Record Research Systemuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261012200834/https://brbpublications.com/products/public-record-research-systemzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.04486nai a22003977i wotm2019-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1121059088  an-us---00aConstitution annotated :banalysis and interpretation of the U.S. Constitution.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aConstitution Annotated 1aWashington, D.C :bLibrary of Congress, Congressional Research Service  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2019-09 and 2020-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Constitution of the United States of America: Analysis and Interpretation (“Constitution Annotated” or “CONAN”) provides a legal analysis and interpretation of the United States Constitution based on a comprehensive review of Supreme Court case law and, where relevant, historical practices that have defined the text of the Constitution. This regularly updated resource is written in “plain English” and useful for a wide audience: from constitutional scholars to those just beginning to learn about the nation’s most important legal document. The Constitution Annotated mainly contains citations to Supreme Court caselaw. Our citations contain hyperlinks to three sources: the Library of Congress, Harvard Law School’s Caselaw Access Project, and the official website for the Supreme Court. How to Cite the Constitution Annotated Following the latest edition of The Bluebook, cite the Constitution Annotated hardbound edition as follows: Cong. Research Serv., Constitution of the United States of America: Analysis and Interpretation (Michael J. Garcia et al. eds., 2012). See The Bluebook: A Uniform System of Citation R. 15, at 149 (Columbia Law Review Ass’n et al. eds., 20th ed. 2015). Cite the Constitution Annotated website as follows: Constitution of the United States: Analysis and Interpretation, Cong. Research Serv. (last visited Aug. 6, 2019), https://constitution.congress.gov. See Bluebook R. 18.2.2, at 182. Cite the Constitution Annotated PDFs as follows: Cong. Research Serv., Constitution of the United States of America: Analysis and Interpretation (Michael J. Garcia et al. eds., 2012), www.congress.gov/content/conan/pdf/GPO-CONAN-2017.pdf. See Bluebook R. 15.9, at 156. Annotations and essays related to impeachment under Articles I, II, and III are found at constitution.congress.gov/browse (in particular, Article II, Section 4) Additional information on impeachment is provided on the websites Beyond the Constitution Annotated: Table of Additional Resources: constitution.congress.gov/resources/additional-resources/… The landing page for the full website is here: constitution.congress.gov/?loclr=bloglawcLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aConstitutionszUnited States. 0aConstitutional lawzUnited States. 0aConstitutional historyzUnited StatesvSources. 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 7aConstitutions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026167 7aLaw materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263512 aLibrary of Congress.bCongressional Research Service,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7906132140uhttps://constitution.congress.gov/about/constitution-annotated/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260528034353/https://constitution.congress.gov/about/constitution-annotated/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03005nai a22004217i wotm2019-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1128106960  an-us---00aCollection James A. Garfield papers.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aJames A. Garfield Papers 1aWashington, DC :bLibrary of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2019-08 and 2019-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe papers of President James A. Garfield, who was assassinated in the first year of his short presidency, have been digitized and are now available online for the first time from the Library of Congress. The Garfield collection includes approximately 80,000 items, mostly dating from 1850 to 1881. Garfield was shot on July 2, 1881, by Charles Guiteau, a mentally disturbed office seeker convinced that Garfield’s death would provide him with a plum diplomatic post. The president’s health would deteriorate for 80 days due to rampant infection after the shooting, and he died on Sept. 19, 1881.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aPresidentszUnited StatesvArchives. 0aCourts-martial and courts of inquiryzUnited States. 0aUniversities and collegeszOhio. 0aUnited StatesxPolitics and governmenty1881-1885. 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection 7aPrimary sources.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20220260792 aLibrary of Congress,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n780890352 aLibrary of Congress.bManuscript Division,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7808064240uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/james-a-garfield-papers/about-this-collection413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260808045558/https://www.loc.gov/collections/james-a-garfield-papers/about-this-collectionzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03083nai a22003977i wotm2019-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1122796728  an-us---00aMental Illness Policy Org. :bunbiased information for policymakers + media.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aMental Illness Policy Org 1aNew York, N.Y.? :bMental Illness Policy Org  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2019-07 and 2019-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aMental Illness Policy Org. was founded in 2011 by DJ Jaffe who was credited with passing NY’s Kendra’s Law and played a major role in passing certain provisions of the Helping Families in Mental Health Crisis Act that were eventually incorporated in the 21st Century CURES Act. The issues facing the seriously mentally ill differ from the problems that affect the much broader population of people who have issues like anxiety and mild depression. The needs of the seriously ill often get lost in the larger dialogue about mental health. Mental Illness Policy Org. brings together the best research and insights from writers and researchers around the world who have studied serious mental illness, and synthesizes their scholarship into actionable policies designed to improve care, save money, and keep public and patients safe. Difference between Mental Illness Policy Org. and other organizations Mental Illness Policy Org. focuses on serious mental illness, not mental health.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aMental health policyzUnited States. 0aMentally illxLegal status, laws, etc.zUnited States. 0aHomeless personsxLegal status, laws, etc.zUnited States. 0aPsychiatric social workzUnited States. 0aHomeless personsxLegal status, laws, etc. 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aUnited States (federal) 6aOrganization website40uhttps://mentalillnesspolicy.org413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261011220418/https://mentalillnesspolicy.orgzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02697nai a22003977i wotm2019-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999ncu|| d o    |0    2eng d  an-us---ae-uk-en00aRepository of Historical Gun Laws. 1a[Durham, North Carolina] :bDuke University  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2019-06 and 2019-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe online Repository of Historical Gun Laws was created by the faculty of the Center for Firearms Law at Duke Law School. “A searchable free database of gun laws from the Middle Ages to 1776 in England and from the Colonial Era to the middle of the twentieth century in the United States. This repository is intended as a resource for scholars and practitioners interested in historical laws concerning firearms and other similar weapons.”cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aFirearmsxLaw and legislationzUnited StatesxHistory. 0aFirearmsxLaw and legislationzGreat BritainxHistory. 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aUnited Kingdom (England) 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aStatutes and codes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110266112 aDuke University.bSchool of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8011294840uhttps://firearmslaw.duke.edu/repository-of-historical-gun-laws413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260913131529/https://firearmslaw.duke.edu/repository-of-historical-gun-lawszBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02286nai a22003857i wotm2019-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999sa || d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1102639388  af------00aAfrican LII. 1aCape Town, South Africa :bAfrican LII  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2019-04 and 2019-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aAfrican LII is a program of the Democratic Governance and Rights Unit at the Department of Public Law, University of Cape Town. The Website provides a Pan-African legal search engine to regional legal materials and maintains sustainable free access to full text of laws, court decisions, administrative decisions, and official gazettes, as well as commentary.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzAfrica. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.zAfrica. 0aForeign & comparative law 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aAfrica 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aLaw materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102635140uhttps://africanlii.org/en/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261109105628/https://africanlii.org/en/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02424nai a22003257i wotm2019-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d00aForeign Law Web Archive. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bLibrary of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2019-03 and 2019-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aForeign law web archive is a collection of foreign legal materials. Included are official gazettes and judicial websites. Many foreign legal materials are now posted online, with some jurisdictions dispensing with a print publication entirely. Certain jurisdictions’ legal materials are challenging to acquire or considered at risk of disappearing from the Web. An official Gazette is a government publication that generally contains the first version of the text of legislation, regulations, rules, legal notices, treaties, and court cases for foreign countries.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aWeb archives.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2004001365 0aLawxComputer network resources. 0aForeign & comparative law 0aInternational / multinational 6aDigital archive / collection2 aLaw Library of Congress (U.S.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n9104313740uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/foreign-law-web-archive/about-this-collection/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260814144646/https://www.loc.gov/collections/foreign-law-web-archive/about-this-collection/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03197nai a22003737i wotm2019-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)110048622700aCenter for International Environmental Law. 1aWashington, D.C :bCenter for International Environmental Law  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2019-02 and 2019-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aSince 1989, the Center for International Environmental Law (CIEL) has worked not only to support communities and defend the environment in the face of looming threats, but to change the very system that gives rise to those threats. CIEL works to build the legal levers for change that will be effective not only for CIEL and its partners, but for others far beyond CIEL’s reach. CIEL’s vision for the world is one where the law reflects the interconnection between humans and the environment, respects the limits of the planet, protects the dignity and equality of each person, and encourages all of earth’s inhabitants to live in balance with each other. In every aspect of its work, CIEL promotes the rule of law; transparent, just and sustainable processes; balanced economic and political power that protects the rights of communities and the environment; democratic inclusion; intra- and inter-generational equity; the interconnectedness of life on the planet; and the intrinsic value of nature and the inherent rights of species and ecosystems.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aEnvironmental law, International.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85044188 0aClimatic changesxLaw and legislation.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2009006504 0aHuman rights.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85026379 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 6aOrganization website2 aCenter for International Environmental Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9502419240uhttps://www.ciel.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261015173320/https://www.ciel.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03078nai a22003617i wotm2019-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)109918267400aInternational Law Institute. 1aWashington, D.C :bInternational Law Institute in cooperation with Georgetown University  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2019-01 and 2019-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe International Law Institute (ILI) has provided legal training to over 38,000 government officials, legal and business professionals, and scholars from 186 countries. ILI also provides technical expertise to developing nations in the design of their own laws and economic infrastructure in efforts to further promote good and efficient governance through the rule of law. ILI presents approximately 40 – 50 core-training programs each year in our facility in Washington, DC. Another 7-15 specialized programs are conducted annually in the U.S. and abroad, at the request of foreign governments, the World Bank, or other sponsoring agencies. In recent years, ILI has presented programs in a wide variety of international settings including: China, Nigeria, Mexico, Singapore, Puerto Rico, Peru, Mauritius, Kazakhstan, Saudi Arabia, Mongolia, Uganda, Ukraine, Russia, Turkmenistan, Philippines and various locations throughout Europe. ILI works closely with many governments across the globe including the US, where we have worked with US Dept. of State in the areas of publishing and training.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aInternational lawxStudy and teaching.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008124031 0aLawxStudy and teaching (Continuing education)0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85075147 0aRule of lawzDeveloping countries. 0aInternational law & organizations 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aInternational / multinational 6aOrganization website40uhttps://www.ili.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260101063623/https://www.ili.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02759nai a22003857i wotm2019-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)33182301  an-us---00aU.S. Environmental Protection Agency.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aUnited States Environmental Protection Agency 1aWashington, DC :bEnvironmental Protection Agency  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2018-12 and 2019-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe mission of United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is to protect human health and the environment and to ensure that Americans have clean air, land and water, as well as to promote national efforts to reduce environmental risks are based on the best available scientific information. All parts of society–communities, individuals, businesses, and state, local and tribal governments–have access to accurate information sufficient to effectively participate in managing human health and environmental risks. Contaminated lands and toxic sites are cleaned up by potentially responsible parties and revitalized. Chemicals in the marketplace are also reviewed for safety.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aEnvironmental protectionzUnited States. 0aEnvironmental policyzUnited States. 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website1 aUnited States.bEnvironmental Protection Agency,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7907959740uhttps://www.epa.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261019130456/http://www.epa.gov/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03293nai a22003497i wotm2019-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999ilu|| w o    |0    2eng d  an-us---00aJurisdocs.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aJurisdocs (Issn 0162-3079) 1a[Chicago] :bAmerican Association of Law Libraries  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2018-11 and 2019-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe triennial publication of the Government Documents Special Interest Section of the American Association of Law Libraries. The Government Documents Special Interest Section as a matter of policy does not sell advertising in JURISDOCS. Materials for publication may be submitted to the editor. Articles may be submitted in any of the following formats: e-mail, fax, or US mail. The copyright to each article that appears in JURISDOCS is held by the author of that article. Reproduction without the author’s permission is forbidden by the Copyright Act of 1976. The editor reserves the right to reject materials submitted for publication. The only exception to this rule is for letters submitted for the “Letters to the Editor” column. This exception provides members an open forum within which to express their opinions. All of the opinions and statements expressed in JURISDOCS represent those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of the GOVDOC SIS or AALL. Editors: Rebecca Kunkel, Reference and Metadata Librarian Rutgers-Newark Law Library Rena K. Seidler, Research and Instructional Services Librarian Ruth Lilly Law Library Indiana University- Robert H. McKinney School of LawcLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLaw librarieszUnited StatesvPeriodicals. 0aGovernment publicationszUnited StatesvPeriodicals.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85140120 0aLaw librarianship, cataloging & information management 0aGovernment information & administrative law 7aNewsletters.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261312 aAmerican Association of Law Libraries,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8002858140uhttps://www.aallnet.org/gdsis/resources-publications/jurisdocs/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20230327155825/https://www.aallnet.org/gdsis/resources-publications/jurisdocs/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03451nai a22003377i wotm2019-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  an-us---00aImmigrant Legal Resource Center.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aImmigration Legal Resource Center 1a[San Francisco] :bImmigrant Legal Resource Center  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2019).  aFeatured between 2018-10 and 2019-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, geographic area codes, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Immigrant Legal Resource Center (ILRC) was founded in 1979 by law professor Bill Ong Hing to help immigration law professionals and advocates develop their expertise, educate immigrant communities to advocate for themselves, and shape immigration law and policy. ILRC also provides legal training, technical assistance, policy updates, and educational materials to attorneys, nonprofit organizations, public defenders, and other immigration advocates, and engages in advocacy and civic engagement efforts designed to advance immigrant rights. The organization’s areas of expertise include asylum, citizenship and naturalization, Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals (DACA ), enforcement, family-based applications for permanent residency, and removal defense for those facing deportation. Currently ILRC is working on the projects and initiatives include CitizenshipWorks, a collaboration with the Immigration Advocates network and Pro Bono Net that helps immigrants apply for citizenship; the Defending Immigrants Partnership, which works with public defender offices and criminal defense organizations on behalf of non-citizens facing criminal charges; and the New Americans Campaign, a national network of legal-service providers, faith-based organizations, businesses, foundations, and community leaders. In addition, through its Technical assistance program, ILRC offers expert legal assistance to legal professionals assisting immigrant clients, while its community resources include know-your-rights” Red Cards” designed to help individuals defend themselves against unconstitutional actions by Immigration and Customs Enforcement.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aEmigration and immigration lawzUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85042791 0aImmigrantsxLegal status, laws, etc.zUnited States. 0aImmigration & citizenship 0aUnited States (federal) 6aOrganization website40uhttps://www.ilrc.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261030050159/https://www.ilrc.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03911nai a22005537i wotm2020-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nmu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1225181683  an-us---00aIDA Treaties Explorer. 1aSanta Fe, N.M :bMuseum of Indian Arts & Culture, National Archives, National Archives Foundation  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2020-09 and 2021-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe IDA Treaties Explore Website provides access to a key context of the agreements and the histories of lands, that is the Ratified Indian Treaties, 374 of the treaties, held by the US National Archives. The land boundaries are mainly from the work of government clerks in the 1890s-1900s to research the history of land transfer treaties or other agreements, in the absence of an official list of all the ratified treaties the US entered into with Native nations. They referred to these as “cessions.” While treaties between indigenous peoples and the United States affect virtually every area in the USA, there is no official list of all the treaties. Beyond their historical and educational value, these treaties are used by tribal advocates in court to protect their rights in land and water disputes. Further documents will be added on an ongoing basis.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aIndians of North AmericavTreatiesxHistoryvSources. 0aIndians of North AmericaxLegal status, laws, etc.vSources. 0aTreaty-making powerzUnited StatesxHistory. 0aIndian land transfersxHistory. 0aLand settlementxHistory. 7aIndians of North AmericaxTreaties.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00969942 7aIndians of North AmericaxLegal status, laws, etc.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00969825 7aIndians of North AmericaxArchives.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00969663 7aIndians of North AmericaxGovernment relations.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00969761 7aTreaty-making power.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01155892 7aIndian land transfers.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00969127 7aLand settlement.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00991305 7aIndigenous peoplesxCivil rights.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00970219 7aHistory.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01411628 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aIndigenous peoples & tribal law 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 0aTribal / Indigenous nations 6aTracker / map / dashboard 7aTreaties.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110267071 aUnited States.bNational Archives and Records Administration,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8417610140uhttps://digitreaties.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261007154743/https://digitreaties.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03044nai a22003737i wotm2020-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)120257542700aCarbon dioxide removal law :ban annotated bibliography of legal materials related to carbon dioxide removal and carbon sequestration and use.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aCarbon Dioxide Removal Law 1aNew York, NY :bSabin Center for Climate Change Law, Columbia Law School, Columbia/SIPA Center on Global Energy Policy  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2020-08 and 2020-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aCarbon Dioxide Removal Law Website launched by the Columbia University provides an annotated bibliography of legal materials related to carbon dioxide removal and of carbon sequestration and use. Currently the site has 530 resources on legal issues related to carbon dioxide removal, including such techniques as: direct air capture; enhanced weathering; afforestation/reforestation; bioenergy with carbon capture and storage; biochar; ocean and coastal carbon dioxide removal; ocean iron fertilization; and soil carbon sequestration. The database also includes 239 legal resources on carbon capture and storage, utilization, and transportation. The site is constantly updated by adding more resources.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCarbon dioxide mitigationxLaw and legislationvBibliography. 0aCarbon sequestrationxLaw and legislationvBibliography. 0aGreenhouse gas mitigationxLaw and legislationvBibliography. 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 7aAnnotated bibliographies.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2019026015 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260812 aSabin Center for Climate Change Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201505923140uhttps://cdrlaw.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261007225113/https://cdrlaw.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02644nai a22003617i wotm2020-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1198500038  an-us---00aStanford Clearinghouse on diversity, equity & inclusion research. 1aStanford, California :bSchool of Law, Stanford University, Robert Crown Law Library  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2020-07 and 2020-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Stanford Law School and Robert Crown Library made a beta version of Clearinghouse Website available for pathbreaking scholarship on diversity, equity, and inclusion in higher education, as well as secondary source materials that provide pedagogically rich opportunities to initiate discussions of the relationship between law and race or other aspects of identity in doctrinal classes. They welcome any feedback or suggestions you have as they work to expand and improve the Clearinghouse over the coming months and years (there is a “connect with us” button on the bottom of the Clearinghouse webpage which can be used to offer comments, suggestions for material to include, etc.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawxStudy and teachingzUnited States. 0aLawxStudy and teaching. 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection40uhttps://sites.google.com/stanford.edu/deiclearinghouse/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260308083504/https://sites.google.com/stanford.edu/deiclearinghouse/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03383nai a22004697i wotm2020-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999ctu|| w o    s0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1192499028  an-us-ct00aWe march on celebrating :b100 years of women's suffrage.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aWe March On Celebrating 1aHartford, Conn :bUniversity of Connecticut School of Law, Thomas J. Meskill Law Library  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2020-06 and 2020-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  a“We March On Celebrating” Website presented by UConn School of Law commemorates the centennial of the nineteenth amendment and recognizes the efforts of the suffrage movement in Connecticut, as well as the key figures and diverse types of work across gender, race, and class that contributed to the suffrage movement and women voting. This anthology contains a historical narrative; the resolute women who planned, marched, addressed opponents, and picketed for more than seven decades. Some of them were women at UConn School of Law. In honor of those participants in the campaign for women’s voting rights and their equality, the exhibit brings to life the long and ongoing movement for women’s suffrage by showcasing many documents and artifacts. More details of the legal background of the suffrage movement are provided by “We March On” Research Guide.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aWomenxSuffragezUnited StatesxHistoryy19th century. 0aWomen's rightszUnited StatesxHistory. 0aAfrican AmericansxSuffragexHistoryy19th century. 0aWomenxSuffrage. 0aAfrican AmericansxSuffrage. 0aUnited StatesxPolitics and governmenty19th century. 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 5aConnecticut 6aDigital archive / collection2 aUniversity of Connecticut.bSchool of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8316349540uhttps://express.adobe.com/page/hGlodA9qmV9Ie/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20240708205221/https://express.adobe.com/page/hGlodA9qmV9Ie/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02765nai a22004337i wotm2020-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999txu|| d o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)79399948  an-us---00aPACER :bPublic access to court electronic records. 1aSan Antonio, Texas :bPACER Service Center  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2020-05 and 2020-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.1 aAccess restricted to subscribersfOnline access with authorization2star  aPACER (acronym) for Public Access to Court Electronic Records) is an electronic public access service of United States federal court documents. It allows users to obtain case and docket information from the United States district courts, United States courts of appeals, and United States courts of bankruptcy court. It holds more than 500 million documents.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCourt recordszUnited StatesvDatabases. 7aCourt records.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00881677 0aCourt administrationzUnited StatesxAutomation. 7aFreedom of information.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00934017 7aCourt administrationxAutomation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00881632 7aCourt recordsxData processing.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00881682 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aUnited States (federal) 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aRecords and briefs.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110265242 aPACER Service Center (U.S.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200206394840uhttps://pacer.uscourts.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261110025525/https://pacer.uscourts.gov/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02643nai a22003857i wotm2020-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999iau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1162872381  an-us---00aRacial justice in the U. S. :boverview.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aRacial Justice in the U. S 1aDes Moines, Iowa :bDrake University Law Library  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2020-04 and 2020-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis guide provides access to educational resources such as the Anti-Defamation League and the National Museum of African American History and Culture on race, racial identity, institutional racial injustice, bias and prejudices with the hope that deep listening to what others share based on mutual respect promotes peaceful dialogue for racial healing. An introduction to community anti-racism coalitions and initiatives paves the way to nurture people for constructive conversations and to deliberately engage in and support the movements of equal justice and systematic social change.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aRacismzUnited States. 0aAfrican AmericansxCivil rights. 0aAfrican AmericansxPolitics and government. 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 6aResearch guide (web)2 aDrake University.bLaw Library,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no202011701440uhttps://libguides.law.drake.edu/racialJustice413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260803091915/https://libguides.law.drake.edu/racialJusticezBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02664nai a22004217i wotm2020-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1156240641  an-us---00aElection Law blog :bthe law of politics and the politics of law: election law, campaign finance, legislation, voting rights, initiatives, redistricting, remedies, and the Supreme Court Rick Hasen's blog.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aElection Law Blog 1aIrvine, California :bUniversity of California, Irvine  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2020-03 and 2020-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis Website provides links to news, stories, and commentaries about major U. S. election law issues from campaign finance to redistricting to election administration along with the blogger’s point of view as well as the legal and political science perspective.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aElection lawzUnited States. 0aCampaign fundsxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aVotingzUnited States. 0aPolitical participationzUnited States. 0aCampaign fundsxLaw and legislation. 0aElections, ethics & government accountability 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aBlogs.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260511 aHasen, Richard L,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200103006740uhttps://electionlawblog.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261104182719/https://electionlawblog.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03271nai a22004097i wotm2020-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuugau|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1152898107  an-us---00aInfluenza (Flu) :bPandemic Influenza. 1aAtlanta, GA :bU.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, National Center for Immunization and Respiratory Diseases NCIRD  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2020-02 and 2020-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aNo verified current address for the original site could be found.  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Center for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) focuses national attention on developing and applying disease control and prevention. Its main goal is to protect communicable and non-communicable public health and safety through the control and prevention of disease, injury, and disability in the US and internationally. The CDC is integral in working with the WHO to implement the International Health Regulations (IHR), a legally binding agreement between 196 countries to prevent, control, and report on the international spread of disease, through initiatives including the Global Disease Detection Program (GDD). The CDC’s Influenza (Flu), Pandemic Influenza Website provides access to regulations and laws that may apply during a pandemic. During a pandemic, certain legal authorities, policies and regulations may apply and serve as the foundation for the US Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) to respond.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aInfluenzazUnited StatesxPrevention. 0aEpidemicszUnited StatesxPrevention. 0aEmergency managementzUnited States. 0aInfluenzaxPrevention. 0aEpidemicsxPrevention. 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aNational security, military & emergency law 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website2 aCenters for Disease Control and Prevention (U.S.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no93011529403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20240717022427/https://www.cdc.gov/flu/pandemic-resources/index.htmzArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.03080nai a22004577i wotm2020-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1147976526  an-us---00aLegal responses to coronavirus (COVID-19). 1aLos Angeles, California :bHugh & Hazel Darling Law Library, UCLA School of Law  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2020-01 and 2020-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis Website provides links to primary sources and high-quality summaries to legal responses to the coronavirus pandemic issued by all levels of governmental units, federal, state, and local. Various legal guidance are updated daily, and some states have laws pre-dating the epidemic but that have become more relevant, such as quarantine statutes and requirements for paid sick leave. At the federal agency level, the focus is placed on agencies related to the provision of healthcare. At the state level, summaries of state laws across the states are highlighted.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCOVID-19 (Disease)xPreventionxGovernment policyzUnited States. 0aCoronavirus infectionsxPreventionxGovernment policyzUnited States. 0aCoronavirus infectionsxPreventionxGovernment policyzCalifornia. 0aCoronavirus infectionsxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aEmergency managementzUnited States. 0aEpidemicszUnited StatesxPrevention. 0aInfluenzaxPrevention. 0aEpidemicsxPrevention. 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aNational security, military & emergency law 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aResearch guide (web)2 aUniversity of California, Los Angeles.bSchool of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7911935140uhttps://libguides.law.ucla.edu/coronavirus413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260514195959/https://libguides.law.ucla.edu/coronaviruszBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02638nai a22004217i wotm2020-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuuxx || w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)31686945400aHG.org :blegal resources.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:ahg.org 1aHouston, Tex.? :bHG.org  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2019-12 and 2020-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aOriginal site no longer available (checked 2026-11-10).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aHG.org, founded in January 1995, is one of the very first online law and government information sites to make law, government and related professional information easily and freely accessible to the legal profession, businesses, and consumers. HG.org includes a directory of law firms and networks covering 195 countries with 5,000 cities in 260 practice areas. Its expert witness directory covers expertise in 1,500 general areas and 700 diseases and medical conditions.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aInternational lawvDirectories. 0aInternational lawxStudy and teachingvDirectories. 0aLawyersvDirectories. 0aCourtsvDirectories. 0aInternational lawxStudy and teaching. 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aInternational law & organizations 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aInternational / multinational 6aPortal or gateway (links to other sites) 7aDirectories.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026087403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260111111624/http://www.hg.org/zArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.02482nai a22003737i wotm2020-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuunju|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1138905278  an-us---00aState constitutions.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aState Constitutional Documents 1aNewark, NJ? :bRutgers Law School  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2019-11 and 2020-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aNo verified current address for the original site could be found.  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aRutgers Law Library provides access to the collection contains the Constitutions, Constitutional convention records, and other related documents for the constitutions of the 50 United States. To access the collection, either select a state to browse the available documents, or search the documents in full text. The collection consists of scanned page images.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aConstitutionszUnited StatesxStates.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2009121607 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aDigital archive / collection 7aConstitutions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026167403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20240925024730/https://njlaw.rutgers.edu/collections/constitutions/index.phpzArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.02825nai a22003737i wotm2020-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110duuuuuuuutxu|| w o    s0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1130059723  an-us---04aThe Thomas Woodward Houghton 50 state ethics guide. 1aAustin, Texas :bTarlton Law Library, Jamail Center for Legal Research, The University of Texas at Austin  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2020).  aFeatured between 2019-10 and 2020-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aOriginal site no longer available (checked 2026-11-10).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Tarlton Law Library of the University of Texas at Austin unveiled a new free resource for legal ethics research. The Thomas Woodward Houghton 50 State Ethics Guide provides links to free options for state codes of attorney and judicial conduct, legal ethics opinions from state and local bars, and selected print and online resources from the American Bar Association.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLegal ethicszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008106765 0aJudicial ethicszUnited States.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008106234 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aResearch guide (web)403Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20230602083156/https://tarlton.law.utexas.edu/ethicszArchived copy (Internet Archive Wayback Machine); the original site is no longer available.423Comparable current resource: GW Law Library: Legal Ethics, State Codesuhttps://law.gwu.libguides.com/ethics/statecodes423Comparable current resource, archived version: GW Law Library: Legal Ethics, State Codesuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261110210941/https://law.gwu.libguides.com/ethics/statecodeszBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03696nai a22005177i wotm2021-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999inu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1287119441  an-us---04aThe COVID crisis in legal education :b2021 Annual survey results.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aThe COVID crisis in legal education 1aBloomington, IN :bCenter for Postsecondary Research, School of Education, Indiana University Bloomington  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2021-08 and 2021-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aLegal education has been challenged, and the LSSSE Annual Report reveals that while the core of legal education remains relatively unchanged, the “intangibles” of law school learning were certainly affected. Above all, the data show unequivocally that our students have been in crisis. Students have struggled to meet their basic needs, with troubling percentages reporting increased worries about housing, financial instability, and even food insecurity. While most made efforts to build relationships with faculty, staff, and classmates, their overall quality of life declined along with opportunities for academic engagement and professional development. The pandemic is a wake up call for the law school to rethink the future of legal education.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawxStudy and teachingzUnited States. 0aLaw studentszUnited StatesxAttitudes. 0aLaw schoolszUnited States. 0aCOVID-19 (Disease)xSocial aspectszUnited States. 0aPublic healthxSocial aspectszUnited States. 0aEpidemicsxSocial aspectszUnited States. 7aLawxStudy and teaching.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00993829 7aLaw studentsxAttitudes.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00994147 7aLaw schools.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00994121 7aCOVID-19 (Disease)xSocial aspects.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01984649 7aPublic healthxSocial aspects.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01082307 7aEpidemicsxSocial aspects.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00914088 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aUnited States (federal) 6aReport or study (single document)2 aIndiana University, Bloomington.bCenter for Postsecondary Research,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200902444440uhttps://lssse.iub.edu/what-we-do/research/annual-results.html413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260718092919/https://lssse.iub.edu/what-we-do/research/annual-results.htmlzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03109nai a22004577i wotm2021-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)234195967  an-us---acl-----00aJustia.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aJustia US Law 1aMountain View, California :bJustia  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2021-07 and 2021-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aJustia is a legal website specializing in information retrieval on law and provides free access to case law, codes, regulations and legal information for lawyers, business, students and consumers world wide in addition to opinion summaries, and other basic legal texts, with paid services for its attorney directory and webhosting. It was founded in 2003 and is one of the largest online databases of legal cases. Justia US law provides the full text of the Annotated US Constitution, as well as recent dockets and selected case filings from the US federal district and appellate courts. In 2007, the New York Times reported that Justia was spending around “$10,000 a month” in order “to copy documents” from the United States Supreme Court and publish them online, to be made available without the public paying fees.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzUnited StatesvCases. 0aLawzLatin AmericavCases. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.zUnited States. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.zLatin America. 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aLatin America 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102617240uhttps://www.justia.com/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261016113907/https://www.justia.com/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02817nai a22003737i wotm2021-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999inu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1265435357  an-us---02aA forgotten history :bthe women who brought us Title IX. 1aBloomington, Indiana :bIndiana University Jerome Hall Law Library  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2021-06 and 2021-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis Oral histories Website collects information about the past from observers and participants in that past. They gather data not available in written records about events, people, decisions, and processes. The point is to preserve, collect, and interpret 20th century history through the medium of first-person testimony. These women played major roles in seeing Title IX become a reality. Some of these women were government employees with responsibility for implementing Title IX. Others–some known, some lesser known–worked with interest groups, especially the National Organizational for Women (NOW) or the Women’s Equity Action League (WEAL), fighting for aggressive enforcement of Title IX.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aUnited States Education Amendments of 1972 Title IXxHistory. 0aSex discrimination in educationxLaw and legislationzUnited StatesxHistory. 0aWomenxEducationxLaw and legislationzUnited StatesxHistory. 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aUnited States (federal) 7aOral histories.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102643140uhttps://www.repository.law.indiana.edu/ohtitleix/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260211064801/https://www.repository.law.indiana.edu/ohtitleix/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02933nai a22004097i wotm2021-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1266330733  an-us---00aIntroduction to law librarianship. 1aMountain View, CA :bPressbooks  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2021-05 and 2021-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis open-access e-textbook, Introduction to Law Librarianship, is the first textbook exclusively devoted to law librarianship. This textbook gives us the tools to critically examine and rethink our profession, our work, and how we educate the next generation of law librarians within these imperfect institutions of historically unequal access. This textbook will allow us to reach beyond our borders to support the growth of the profession around the world. Librarianship is one of the oldest professions in the world, however, Law Librarianship is a relatively new profession. The varied and specialized nature of law libraries and law librarianship are uniquely positioned in the United States. This textbook will provide new perspectives on traditional activities with greater creativity and innovation.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLaw librarieszUnited States. 0aLaw librarianszUnited States. 0aLegal researchzUnited States. 7aLaw libraries.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00994020 7aLaw librarians.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00994016 7aLegal research.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00995522 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aLaw librarianship, cataloging & information management 0aLegal research & reference tools 7aTextbooks.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402619140uhttps://pressbooks.pub/lawlibrarianship/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20251207222451/https://pressbooks.pub/lawlibrarianship/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02630nai a22004097i wotm2021-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xx || w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1258689815  an-us---00aInitiative on Climate Risk and Resilience Law. 1aUnited States :bInitiative on Climate Risk and Resilience Law  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2021-04 and 2021-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aAs the climate crisis intensifies, it is crucial that policymakers strengthen protections from the dangers of climate change to our nation’s financial system and the millions of people who rely on it to sustain the American economy. The consequences of climate change become more visible with each passing year. The Initiative on Climate Risk and Resilience Law Website (ICRRL) is an outcome of collaborative efforts of experts to advocate integrated policies on the climate crisis.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aClimatic changesxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aClimatic changesxEconomic aspectszUnited States. 0aClimatic changesxSocial aspectszUnited States. 0aEnergy industriesxEnvironmental aspectszUnited States. 0aDisclosure of informationxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aResilience (Ecology)zUnited States. 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aUnited States (federal) 6aOrganization website40uhttps://www.icrrl.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260808153215/https://www.icrrl.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03628nai a22003977i wotm2021-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999enk|| d o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)661092701  ae-uk---00aLegislation.gov.uk. 1aLondon :bNational Archives on behalf of HM Government  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2021-03 and 2021-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aLegislaiton.gov.uk, maintained by the National Archives, is a free and reliable source to research UK legislation. It carries all primary legislation from 1988 and secondary legislation from 1987. Although there is no equivalent of a statutory code in the UK, users may consult the “Latest Available (Revised) Version” to find the law with the most recent changes. The United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Island (UK) contains four jurisdictions: England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland. The UK Parliament in Westminster, London retains the exclusive power to pass legislation for all jurisdictions on “reserved matters”-for example, the making of peace or war, defense, international trade, foreign relations and other matters of public concern. Wales, Scotland and Northern Island have their own legislative bodies and may pass laws on issues that are known as “devolved matters.” UK legislation are divided into two main categories: primary and secondary legislation. The laws passed by the legislative bodies of the UK (e.g., Acts of the UK Parliament, Scottish Parliament, Welsh Parliament and Northern Ireland Assembly) are “primary legislation” or “statutes”. Secondary legislation (also known as “subordinate” or “delegated” legislation) are equivalent to administrative regulations in the United States-they are specialized rules and regulations issued by ministers or governmental entities with authority delegated to them by an Act of Parliament.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzGreat BritainvDatabases. 0aLegislationzGreat BritainvDatabases. 0aForeign & comparative law 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aUnited Kingdom 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aStatutes and codes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110266112 aNational Archives (Great Britain),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nr200301288840uhttps://www.legislation.gov.uk/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261108185908/https://www.legislation.gov.uk/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02749nai a22004577i wotm2021-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999ke || w o    |0    2eng d  af------00aAfrican Journal of International Economic Law. 1aNairobi, Kenya :bSheria Publishing House  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2021-02 and 2021-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe African Journal of International Economic Law, (AfJIEL) is an open access peer review journal made available on the Website of the African International Economic Law Network, (AfIELN). The focus of the journal is on international economic law, international trade law, regional trade and other topics relating to Africa and the Global South.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLaw and economic developmentzAfricavPeriodicals. 0aInternational lawzDeveloping countriesvPeriodicals. 7aLaw and economic development.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00993898 7aInternational law.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00976984 7aInternational economic relations.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00976891 7aEconomic policy.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00902025 7aAfrica.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01239509 7aDeveloping countries.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01242969 0aInternational law & organizations 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aLegal scholarship & law journals 0aForeign (single country or region) 0aInternational / multinational 5aAfrica 7aLaw reviews.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102635240uhttps://www.afronomicslaw.org/journal413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261108162645/https://www.afronomicslaw.org/journalzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03262nai a22004337i wotm2021-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999azu|| w o    s0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1244817440  an-us---00aPolice practices resources :bNational Calendar of Events and Resources related to Police Practices.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aPolice Practices Resources 1aTucson, Ariz :bUniversity of Arizona, College of Law Library  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2021-01 and 2021-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Website of National Calendar of Events and Resources related to Police Practices is a collaborative project between law librarians and the ABA Legal Education Police Practices Consortium. The charge of the project is to “aim to contribute to the national effort examining and addressing legal issues in policing and public safety, including conduct, oversight, and the evolving nature of police work.” The selective enforcement of laws against people of color has resulted in a disproportionate effect on certain communities. The population of this calendar relies on colleagues around the country to submit events to the calendar. Events can be submitted in two ways: 1) Complete the short Events Submission Form, or 2) Email the event details to LawCatCollaborative. You can access the calendar in a web browser and add it to your outlook calendar (iCal). You are welcome to embed the calendar into your website or LibGuide as well.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aPolice brutalityzUnited States. 0aPolice misconductzUnited States. 0aDiscrimination in law enforcementzUnited States. 0aRacismzUnited States. 0aPolice-community relationszUnited States. 0aPolice administrationzUnited States. 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aPortal or gateway (links to other sites)40uhttps://lawlibrary.arizona.edu/police-practices-resources413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250419231606/http://lawlibrary.arizona.edu/police-practices-resourceszBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03200nai a22004577i wotm2021-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)57995521  an-us---00aLegal Momentum :bthe Women's Legal Defense and Education Fund.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aLegal Momentum 1aNew York, NY :bLegal Momentum  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2020-12 and 2021-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aLegal Momentum, a 501(c)3 nonprofit, founded in 1970 is the oldest legal advocacy group for women in the United States, formerly known as NOW Legal Defense and Education Fund. Legal Momentum is a liberal multi-issue organization dedicated to advancing women’s rights across the country. Their vision is for all women and girls to realize their full potential and advances the rights of women and girls, as well as equity in education, the workplace, and the courts by using the power of the law and creating innovative public policy. Along with a mission focused on ensuring economic and personal security for all women and girls, Legal Momentum provides an expert legal voice to seek justice for women and has made historic and enduring contributions to the rights and opportunities available to women through their litigation initiatives.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aWomenxLegal status, laws, etc.zUnited StatesvCases. 0aWomen's rightszUnited StatesvCases. 0aWomenxEducationzUnited States. 0aFeminist jurisprudencezUnited States. 0aEqual rights amendmentszUnited States. 0aSex discrimination against womenxLaw and legislation. 0aWomenxEducation. 0aWomenxLegal status, laws, etc. 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 6aOrganization website2 aLegal Momentum (Organization),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201200782140uhttps://www.legalmomentum.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261030040350/https://www.legalmomentum.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03144nai a22004217i wotm2021-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999lu || d o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)40689896  ae------00aEUR-Lex :baccess to European Union Law.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aEUR-Lex 1aLuxembourg :bPublications Office of the European Union  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2020-11 and 2021-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aEUR-Lex contains more than 50,000 pages of EU law covering external relations, secondary and supplementary legislation, and a series of non-binding acts, including all Official Journal issues of the last 45 days, EU treaties, recent judgements of the Court of Justice and consolidated legal acts. The following EU legal documents are available at the EUR-Lex Website. - Treaties - Legal acts from EU institutions - Preparatory documents related to EU legislation - EU case-law - International agreements - EFTA documents - References to national case-law related to EU law. How to use EUR-Lex EUR-Lex offers a wide range of options to search within its various collections as well as numerous functions catering to different needs. You can also view up to three linguistic versions of the same document simultaneously. Furthermore, you can create a user account to customize the website to your specific needs and benefit from extra features.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzEuropean Union countries. 0aLegislationzEuropean Union countries. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.zEuropean Union countries. 0aForeign & comparative law 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aEuropean Union 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aStatutes and codes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102661140uhttps://eur-lex.europa.eu/en/index.htm413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20140310231511/https://eur-lex.europa.eu/en/index.htmzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03414nai a22004337i wotm2021-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999onc|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)122094956700aThird world approaches to international law review :bTWAIL review.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aThird World Approaches to International Law Review 1aWindsor, Canada :bUniversity of Windsor, Faculty of Law  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2021).  aFeatured between 2020-10 and 2021-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Third World Approaches to International Law Review (TWAILR) is intended to furthering the aims of the TWAIL network and to support the decolonisation of the lived realities of the peoples of the Global South and the radical transformation of the international order which governs their lives. The Third World Approaches to International Law (TWAIL) network was founded in 1996. ‘Third World’ refers to that expansive and usually subordinated socio-political geography seen as ‘non-aligned belonging neither to the ‘free’ nor to the ‘communist’ world. Since the first TWAIL meeting in 1997, the focus of TWAILers has placed on the cross-examined international law agendas, neutrality and universality associated with imperialism. In this rapidly globalizing world, B.S. Chimni mentioned that “the threat of ‘recolonisation’ has continued to haunt the Third World. Facing this reality, a new set of tools had to be developed to address the material and ethical concerns of third world peoples.”cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aInternational lawzDeveloping countriesvPeriodicals. 0aComparative lawvPeriodicals. 7aInternational law.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00976984 7aComparative law.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00871350 7aConflict of laws.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00874831 7aLaw reviews.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00994120 7aDeveloping countries.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01242969 0aInternational law & organizations 0aLegal scholarship & law journals 0aInternational / multinational 7aLaw reviews.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263522 aUniversity of Windsor.bFaculty of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8202822740uhttps://twailr.com/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261019002349/https://twailr.com/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03010nai a22004457i wotm2022-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999miu|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)5188216900aAnimal Legal and Historical Center Website. 1aEast Lansing, Mich :bMichigan State University College of Law  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2022-08 and 2022-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aIn March 2022, the Animal Legal & Historical Center celebrated its 20th anniversary. Over the years, with the help of many individuals, they’ve added thousands of files that are accessed across the globe. Included are full text cases (US, Historical and UK), U.S. statutes, and legal articles addressing a wide variety of animal topics. This is the best way for non-lawyers to access the information. The international collection continues to expand. Please share your comments at animallaw@law.msu.edu. Your feedback helps ensure this site’s growth and presence for years to come.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aAnimal welfarexLaw and legislationvCases. 0aWildlife conservationxLaw and legislationvCases. 0aAnimal rightsvCases. 0aAnimal welfarexLaw and legislation. 0aWildlife conservationxLaw and legislation. 0aAnimalsxLaw and legislation. 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261722 aMichigan State University.bCollege of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200405849740uhttps://www.animallaw.info/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261017151410/https://www.animallaw.info/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02776nai a22003857i wotm2022-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1295642076  an------as------00aAmerican Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples. 1aWashington, DC, USA :bOrganization of American States  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2022-07 and 2022-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe American Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples was adopted at the Third Plenary Session of the Organization of American States (OAS) General Secretariat held on June 15, 2016. The indigenous peoples of the Americas are culturally distinct groups who maintain an ancestral bond to the lands where they live or wish to live. Also, the Indigenous peoples of the Americans have the right to live in harmony with nature and to a healthy, safe, and sustainable environment, essential conditions for the full enjoyment of the right to life, to their spirituality, world view and to collective well-being.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 7aHuman rights.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00963285 7aIndigenous peoplesxCivil rights.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00970219 7aAmerica.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01239786 0aIndigenous peoples & tribal law 0aHuman rights 0aInternational / multinational 7aLaw materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263512 aOrganization of American States.bGeneral Assembly,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7913079840uhttps://www.oas.org/en/sare/documents/DecAmIND.pdf413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260925220027/https://www.oas.org/en/sare/documents/DecAmIND.pdfzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02326nai a22003617i wotm2022-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cou|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)321173752  an-us---00aNational Conference of State Legislatures. 1aDenver, CO ; Washington, DC :bNational Conference of State Legislatures  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2022-06 and 2022-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe National Conference of State Legislatures (NCSL) founded in 1975 provides information about legislatures in the states, territories and commonwealths of the U.S. Its mission is to advance the effectiveness, independence, and integrity of legislatures and to improve the operations and management of state legislatures, and the effectiveness of legislators and legislative staff.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aState governmentszUnited States. 0aParliamentary practicezUnited States. 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aOrganization website2 aNational Conference of State Legislatures,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8104214240uhttps://www.ncsl.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261102045451/https://www.ncsl.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02636nai a22003737i wotm2022-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1344311516  an-us---00aNonprofit law blogs. 1aSan Francisco, CA :bNEO Law Group  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2022-05 and 2022-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aNonprofit law blogs website hosted by Gene Takagi, a Principal at NEO Law Group, provides practical and responsive counsel that helps nonprofits and their leaders further their missions and values. NEO Law Group believes that healthy nonprofits are more effective at creating positive change. NEO Law Group represented over 800 nonprofits over the past 15 years on matters including formations, fiscal sponsorship, governance, collaborations, affiliations, mergers, advocacy, earned income, and general nonprofit corporate and tax law compliance. NEO Law Group also presented on nonprofit legal topics to charities, private foundations, bar associations, and CPA associations throughout the country.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aNonprofit organizationsxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aLawzUnited StatesxComputer network resources. 0aNonprofit organizationsxLaw and legislation. 0aLawxComputer network resources. 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aUnited States (federal) 7aBlogs.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402605140uhttps://nonprofitlawblog.com/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261029062527/https://nonprofitlawblog.com/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03145nai a22003857i wotm2022-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)763416368  an-us---00aCourt Listener. 1aEmeryville, California :bFree Law Project  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2022-03 and 2022-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Court Listener Website provides public access to primary legal materials on the Internet for educational, charitable, and scientific purposes to the benefit of the general public and the public interest as well as to technologies useful for academic research on corpora and legal systems. Free Law Project is a California non-profit public benefit corporation and a federally-recognized 501(c)(3) public charity whose specific purposes are primarily to create an open ecosystem for legal research and materials and to carry on other charitable activities associated with these purposes, including, but not limited to, publications, meetings, conferences, trainings, educational seminars, and the issuance of grants and other financial support to educational institutions, foundations, and other organizations exclusively for educational, charitable, and scientific purposes as allowed by law. Free Law Project also has several initiatives that collect and share legal information, including the largest collection of American oral argument audio, daily collection of new legal opinions from 200 United States courts and administrative bodies.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.zUnited States. 0aCircuit courtszUnited States. 0aLawzUnited StatesvCases. 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102617240uhttps://courtlistener.com/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261019204315/http://courtlistener.com/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03174nai a22004817i wotm2022-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1097192950  an-us-ca00aLocating the law :ba handbook for non-law librarians.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aLocating the law 1aLos Angeles, California :bSouthern California Association of Law Libraries  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2022-02 and 2022-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Committee of the Southern California Association of Law Libraries (SCALL) made accessible a revised version of their comprehensive legal reference resource Locating the Law: A Handbook for Non-Law Librarians, 6th ed. The handbook provides ready access to a reliable legal research reference resource to assist you in reading legal citations and how to conduct legal research.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLegal researchzUnited StatesvHandbooks, manuals, etc. 0aLegal researchzCaliforniavHandbooks, manuals, etc. 0aLawzUnited StatesvBibliography. 0aLawzCaliforniavBibliography. 7aLaw.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00993678 7aLegal research.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00995522 7aCalifornia.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204928 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aLaw librarianship, cataloging & information management 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 5aCalifornia 7aHandbooks and manuals.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026109 7aBibliographies.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260482 aSouthern California Association of Law Libraries,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8780537140uhttps://scallnet.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/Locating-the-Law-Sixth-Edition-Complete-with-links.pdf413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250501184422/http://scallnet.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/03/Locating-the-Law-Sixth-Edition-Complete-with-links.pdfzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02401nai a22003617i wotm2022-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999sz || d o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)131267531100aUN Archives Geneva. 1aGeneva, Switzerland :bUnited Nations Library & Archive  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2022-01 and 2022-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe UN Archives Geneva Website provides access to the fonds and collections managed by the United Nations Library and Archives in Geneva, including the archives of the United Nations in Geneva, the League of Nations (1919-1946), international peace movements (from 1870), and private papers. It offers the possibility to search both the description of files or archival documents and in the full text of archival documents that have been digitized.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aInternational lawxHistoryvDatabases. 0aInternational law & organizations 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aInternational / multinational 6aDigital archive / collection 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260812 aUnited Nations Office at Geneva,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9303370840uhttps://archives.ungeneva.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260906162820/https://archives.ungeneva.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03311nai a22004577i wotm2022-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1308956432  an-us---00aAmerican state papers, 1789-1838, Thirty-eight volumes.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aAmerican State Papers 1aWashington, DC :bLibrary of Congress  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2021-12 and 2022-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe American State Papers, comprising a total of thirty-eight physical volumes, is a collection of the legislative and executive documents of Congress during the period 1789 to 1838. The collection includes documents that cover the critical historical gap from 1789 to the printing of the first volume of the U.S. Serial Set in 1817. There are 6,278 documents in 38 volumes. They are arranged into ten topical classes or series as follows: I. Foreign Relations II. Indian Affairs III. Finances IV. Commerce and Navigation V. Military Affairs VI. Naval Affairs VII. Post Office Department VIII. Public Lands IX. Claims X. MiscellaneouscLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aGovernment publicationszUnited States. 0aArchiveszUnited States. 0aPublic lawzUnited StatesvSources. 0aUnited StatesxHistoryy1789-1865vSources. 0aUnited StatesxPolitics and governmenty1815-1861. 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (federal) 7aLegislative materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026362 7aPrimary sources.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20220260792 aLibrary of Congress,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n780890351 aUnited States.bCongress,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7900686540uhttps://www.loc.gov/collections/century-of-lawmaking/articles-and-essays/statutes-and-documents/american-state-papers/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260617151341/https://www.loc.gov/collections/century-of-lawmaking/articles-and-essays/statutes-and-documents/american-state-papers/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03225nai a22003857i wotm2022-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999vau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)922580936  an-us---00aTax notes (Falls Church, Virginia).1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aTax Notes 1aFalls Church, VA :bTax Analysts  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2021-11 and 2022-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.1 aAccess restricted to subscribersfOnline access with authorization2star  aTax Notes is a portfolio of publications offered by Tax Analysts, a nonprofit tax publisher. It provides comprehensive and impartial coverage of tax news, while its commentary contributes important voices to the discussion and understanding of tax policy. Founded in 1970, Tax Analysts was created to foster free, open, and informed discussion about taxation. In 1972 Tax Analysts published ax Notes Federal, its first weekly journal, featuring news, commentary, and analysis on federal taxation. In 1989 Tax Analysts added Tax Notes International, a weekly magazine focused on international taxation. Tax Notes State State rounded out the weekly portfolio in 1991. Each magazine offers best-in-class tax commentary and analysis on the latest changes in tax law and policy, as well as on court opinions, legislative action, and revenue rulings. Tax Notes has continued to innovate through the years, adding the online daily news services Tax Notes Today Federal, Tax Notes Today International, and Tax Notes Today State between 1987 and 1991. Tax Notes also provides several research and reference tools, as well as specialized services focusing on exempt organizations, state tax audit guidance, and international tax treaties.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aTaxationxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aTax administration and procedurezUnited States. 0aTaxationxLaw and legislation. 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aPeriodicals.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402613940uhttps://www.taxnotes.com/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260921131524/https://www.taxnotes.com/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03523nai a22004697i wotm2022-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1294643110  an-us---00aStrategies and techniques for Integrating diversity, equity and inclusion into the core law curriculum :bcomprehensive guide to DEI pedagogy, course planning, and classroom practice.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aStrategies and techniques for Integrating diversity, equity and inclusion into the core law curriculum 1aNew York :bWolters Kluwer  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2021-10 and 2022-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aProfessor Teri A. McMurtry-Chubb at the University of Illinois Chicago School of Law has authored a book about the strategies to incorporate diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) into classrooms. The focus of this publication is on learning outcomes and assessments, and course planning templates for each course in the core law curriculum, and racial trauma-informed teaching approaches. Each chapter also includes FAQs and discussion questions to work through for the course planning and DEI curricular initiatives to transform the way we think, teach, learn and act such that all experiences and ways of being are handled with fairness and justice.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLaw schoolsxCurriculazUnited States. 0aMulticultural educationzUnited States. 0aIntercultural communicationzUnited States. 0aCommunication in educationzUnited States. 0aAnti-racismxStudy and teachingzUnited States. 0aSocial justicexStudy and teachingzUnited States. 7aLawxStudy and teaching.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00993829 7aLaw schoolsxCurricula.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00994130 7aMulticultural education.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01028816 7aIntercultural communication.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00976084 7aCommunication in education.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00870133 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 7aHandbooks and manuals.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402610940uhttps://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0687/3566/4408/files/STDEI_1E.pdf?v=1698127623413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20230514134448/https://www.aspenpublishing.com/File%20Library/Resources/Faculty/Strategies-Techniques-Teaching/STDEI_1e.pdfzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03020nai a22004457i wotm2022-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999ncu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1052647359  an-us---00aJudicature. 1aDurham, NC :bDuke University, Center for Judicial Studies  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2022).  aFeatured between 2021-09 and 2022-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aDuke Law School took on the publication of Judicature in May 2015 after the American Judicature Society (AJS) disbanded after promoting the interests of justice and judiciary more than a century. Judicature is published under the auspices of the Bolch Judicial Institute at Duke Law School, which was established in 2018. Its missions are to study the rule of law as well as advancing legal research for deepening our understanding of the law to improve the administration of justice. In fall 2021, Judicature International launches online to provide judges around the globe with a forum for sharing commentary, scholarship, empirical research, opinion, and other content exploring issues of common concern to judges around the world.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzUnited StatesvPeriodicals. 0aInternational lawvPeriodicals. 0aGlobalizationvPeriodicals. 7aLaw.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00993678 7aInternational law.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00976984 7aGlobalization.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00943532 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aLegal scholarship & law journals 0aUnited States (federal) 0aInternational / multinational 7aPeriodicals.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261392 aDuke University.bSchool of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8011294840uhttps://judicature.duke.edu/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260719163310/https://judicature.duke.edu/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03213nai a22004097i wotm2023-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999vau|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)680716346  an-us---00aCourt Statistics Project. 1aWilliamsburg, VA :bNational Center for State Courts  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured between 2023-12 and 2024-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Court Statistics Project (CSP) provides a systematic means to develop a valid, uniform, and complete statistical database that details the operation of state court systems. It provides high-quality, baseline information on state court structure, jurisdiction, reporting practices, and caseload volume and trends. Effective management and planning at the local, state, and national levels depend on accurate, consistent, and comparable information to assess the business of the state courts, identify trends in litigation, and estimate future levels of demand. The CSP fulfills the vital role of translating diverse state court caseload statistics into a common framework that all states use when establishing their respective goals and policies. Information for the CSP’s national caseload databases comes from published and unpublished sources supplied by state court administrators and appellate court clerks.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aState courtszUnited StatesvStatisticsvDatabases. 7aState courts.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01131767 7aJudicial statisticsxU.S. states.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00984743 7aCourt administrationxU.S. states.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00881653 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aStatistics.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026181 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260812 aNational Center for State Courts,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7912962740uhttps://www.ncsc.org/resources-courts/data413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250511140706/https://www.courtstatistics.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02971nai a22003857i wotm2023-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999miu|| w o    s0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1407075934  an-us---00aData for defenders. 1aAnn Arbor, Michigan :bUniversity of Michigan Law School  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured between 2023-11 and 2023-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe MDefenders Project is a faculty-sponsored organization at the University of Michigan Law School designed to (a) help current law students figure out if public defense is the right career path for them; (b) provide a nurturing and supportive community for aspiring public defenders; (c) teach aspiring public defenders skills to help them succeed as public defenders; (d) connect University of Michigan alumni who are doing public defense work with one another and provide them with helpful resources; and (e) help connect students and alumni interested in public defense jobs with employers who are looking to hire public defenders. This project is founded on the belief that high-quality defense work starts and ends with people: giving context to the circumstances of the people we represent, giving consideration to the ways that people think and learn, and giving the right information to the people tasked with decision-making inside the courtroom.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aEyewitness identificationzUnited States. 0aPrejudiceszUnited States. 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aOrganization website2 aUniversity of Michigan.bLaw School,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8211698140uhttps://datafordefenders.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260925150102/https://datafordefenders.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03223nai a22003737i wotm2023-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999sz || w o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)86261198300aUnderstanding the Indigenous and Tribal People Convention, 1989 (No. 169) :bhandbook for ILO Tripartite Constituents. 1aGeneva :bInternational Labour Office  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured between 2023-10 and 2023-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe handbook is written by Birgitte Feiring, former Chief Technical Adviser of the ILO’s Programme to Promote ILO Convention No. 169 (PRO 169). This handbook aims to answer some of the essential questions of ILO constituents regarding the Indigenous and Tribal Peoples Convention, 1989 (No. 169). Convention No. 169 is a unique Convention. Adopted by the International Labour Conference (ILC) in 1989, it represents a consensus reached by ILO tripartite constituents. Indigenous and tribal peoples are among the vulnerable groups of concern to the ILO to promote social justice, internationally recognized human and labour rights and Decent Work. Takes a holistic approach covering a wide range of issues that affect the lives and well-being of these peoples and has become a global reference point with impact on governance and development policies.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 7aIndigenous and Tribal Peoples Convention (1989 June 27)2fast0(OCoLC)fst01774054 7aIndigenous peoples (International law)2fast0(OCoLC)fst01737039 7aIndigenous peoplesxLegal status, laws, etc.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00970247 0aIndigenous peoples & tribal law 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 7aHandbooks and manuals.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261092 aInternational Labour Office,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7908635140uhttps://www.ilo.org/sites/default/files/wcmsp5/groups/public/@ed_norm/@normes/documents/publication/wcms_205225.pdf413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250405105738/https://www.ilo.org/sites/default/files/wcmsp5/groups/public/@ed_norm/@normes/documents/publication/wcms_205225.pdfzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02531nai a22003617i wotm2023-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xxu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1393271946  an-us---00aPatent law :bcases, problems, and materials. 1aUnited States :bJonathan S. Masur & Lisa Larrimore Ouellette  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured 2023-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe 3rd Edition of Patent Law: Cases, Problems, and Materials is a public accessible patent law casebook. The full casebook PDF is downloadable online free of charge, and a print version is available at-cost from Amazon. The focus of this casebook is on bringing conceptual clarity to the details of modern patent practice, while also placing patent law in its social context. Many practice problems are introduced throughout the casebook to apply patent doctrines to fact patterns from real cases and to hypotheticals constructed to isolate and explain difficult concepts.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aPatent laws and legislationzUnited States. 0aPatent laws and legislationzUnited StatesvCases. 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 0aUnited States (federal) 7aCasebooks (Law).2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026115 7aTextbooks.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402619140uhttps://www.patentcasebook.org413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260606201417/https://www.patentcasebook.orgzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03211nai a22004457i wotm2023-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999enk|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1392039048  aa-is---00aMapping civilian harm claims against Israel and the Palestinian Authority before Israeli courts :b[Interactive map].1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aMapping civilian harm claims against Israel and the Palestinian Authority before Israeli courts 1aLondon :bCeasefire Centre for Civilian Rights  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured between 2023-07 and 2023-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis interactive map database jointly developed by Professor Gilat Bachar at Temple University Beasley School of Law and Dr. Haim Abraham at the University College London, and the NGO, Ceasefire, Center for Civilian Rights. The database presents over 450 Israeli court cases in which civilians sought compensation under tort law for loss of life, bodily injury, and property damage inflicted during armed activities. Included cases are Palestinian civilians who were injured by Israeli security forces in the Gaza Strip and the West Bank in addition to claims of other nationals, as well as cases in which Israeli civilians sought compensation against Hamas and the Palestinian Authority for losses incurred during hostilities for the facilitation of reparations and reconciliation.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aWar damage compensationxLaw and legislationvCases. 0aLiability for human rights violationsvCases. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.zIsrael. 0aWar damage compensationxLaw and legislation. 0aHuman rights 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aNational security, military & emergency law 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aIsrael 5aPalestinian territories 6aTracker / map / dashboard 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102617240uhttps://civilian-harm-map.ceasefire.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260608210508/https://civilian-harm-map.ceasefire.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02692nai a22004217i wotm2023-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1381277518  an-us---00aGoverning rules and responsibilities. 1aWashington, D.C :bU.S. Small Business Administration  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured 2023-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Governing Rules and Responsibilities Website outlines regulations dealing with government contracting programs for small businesses and provides access to the Federal Acquisition Regulation for the government’s purchasing process. You will learn about the contract provisions to protect the integrity of the government procurement process.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aGovernment purchasingxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aPublic contractszUnited States. 0aSmall businessxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aGovernment purchasingxLaw and legislation. 0aSmall businessxLaw and legislation. 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website 7aAdministrative regulations.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110260301 aUnited States.bSmall Business Administration,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7909932240uhttps://www.sba.gov/counseling/governing-rules-and-responsibilities/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261017073705/https://www.sba.gov/counseling/governing-rules-and-responsibilities/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02721nai a22003977i wotm2023-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999sz || d o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)67004574300aWIPO Lex. 1aGeneva, Switzerland :bWorld Intellectual Property Organization, WIPO  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured 2023-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe World Intellectual Property Organization (WIPO) is the global forum for intellectual property (IP) services, policy, and information. The mission of the organization is to lead the development of a balanced and effective international IP system. The WIPO Lex Database is a one-stop search for the intellectual property (IP) laws, regulations, WIPO-administered treaties and IP‑related treaties, and IP judgments. The WIPO Lex Website features related information to analyze and interpret these laws and treaties.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aIntellectual propertyvCases. 0aAntitrust lawvCases. 0aIntellectual property infringementxLaw and legislationvCases. 0aIntellectual property infringementxLaw and legislation. 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 0aInternational law & organizations 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aLaw materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263512 aWorld Intellectual Property Organization,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7910336440uhttps://www.wipo.int/en/web/wipolex/index413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261030084355/https://www.wipo.int/en/web/wipolex/indexzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02790nai a22003977i wotm2023-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999flu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1374880449  an-us---00aNational Freedom of Information Coalition. 1aFlorida :bNational Freedom of Information Coalition  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured 2023-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe National Freedom of Information Coalition (NFOIC) is a nonprofit and nonpartisan resource to protect our right to open government by ensuring state and local governments and public institutions have laws, policies and procedures to facilitate press and public access to information and proceedings. The mission of NFOIC is to support and empower state coalitions in order to improve the laws, judicial remedies, and practice of government information dissemination at the state and local levels. NFOIC is proactively working to integrate new voices into the government transparency world, recognizing that information is critical to empowering marginalized groups, and that new perspectives are essential to healthy civic engagement and democracy.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aFreedom of informationzUnited States. 0aGovernment informationxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aPublic recordsxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aPublic recordsxAccess controlzUnited States. 0aPublic recordsxLaw and legislation. 0aPublic recordsxAccess control. 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aOrganization website40uhttps://www.nfoic.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261029063939/https://www.nfoic.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03336nai a22004337i wotm2023-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999mau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1371333891  an-us---00aDigital media law project. 1aCambridge, MA :bBerkman Center for Internet & Society  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured 2023-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Digital Media Law Project (DMLP) was founded with the mission through five core initiatives: (1) its detailed Legal Guide on media and business law topics for non-lawyers; (2) its searchable Database of Legal Threats directed at online publishers; (3) its nationwide attorney referral service, the Online Media Legal Network; (4) its Research & Response initiative to address breaking issues and trends in digital media law; and (5) its regularly-updated Blog and Newsletter on current issues in media law, technology law and journalism. Also the DMLP publishes a comprehensive legal guide for individuals and organizations involved in independent online publishing and journalism. The guide covers topics ranging from how to form a business to how to use freedom of information and open meetings laws to get access to information, meetings, and governmental records, as well as other legal subjects such as risks associated with online publication, including discussion of defamation and privacy torts; legal issues related to news gathering; use of copyrighted and trademarked materials; and special risks associated with covering elections.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aDigital mediaxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aInternetxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aFreedom of expressionzUnited States. 0aTelecommunicationxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aDigital mediaxLaw and legislation. 0aInternetxLaw and legislation. 0aTelecommunicationxLaw and legislation. 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 6aOrganization website 7aGuidebooks.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402610840uhttps://www.dmlp.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260713013532/https://www.dmlp.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02554nai a22003617i wotm2023-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)136909647400aCenter for Reproductive Rights. 1aNew York :bHeadquarters Center for Reproductive Rights  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured between 2022-10 and 2023-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Center for Reproductive Rights is a global human rights organization of lawyers and advocates who can apply their expertise and experience in human rights, comparative, and constitutional law across five continents. The Center is the only global legal advocacy organization dedicated to advancing reproductive rights as fundamental human rights around the world and envisions a world where every person participates with dignity as an equal member of society, regardless of gender; where every woman is free to decide whether or when to have children and whether to get married; where access to quality reproductive health care is guaranteed; and where every woman can make these decisions free from coercion or discrimination.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aHuman reproductionxLaw and legislation. 0aAbortionxLaw and legislation. 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aHuman rights 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aInternational / multinational 6aOrganization website40uhttps://reproductiverights.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261101175529/https://reproductiverights.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03251nai a22003977i wotm2023-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cou|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1356865954  an-us---00aCryptocurrency 2022 Legislation. 1aDenver, Colo :bNational Conference of State Legislatures  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2023).  aFeatured between 2022-09 and 2023-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe National Conference of State Legislatures (NCSL) complied the cryptocurrency enacted legislation of the thirty-seven states in the 2022 legislative session. Digital currencies are a medium of exchange but are not regular money. Digital currencies are stored in digital wallets, which are software or apps installed by users on their computer or mobile device. Each digital wallet contains encrypted information, called public and private keys, that is used to send and receive the digital currency. All digital currency transactions are recorded in a virtual public ledger called the “blockchain,” which is maintained by digital currency “miners.” These miners can be anyone, anywhere in the world, who is willing to invest in the specialized computer hardware needed to rapidly process complex computations. Miners are awarded digital currency, like Bitcoin, Ripple, Dogecoin, and Litecoin, in exchange for verifying each transaction and adding it to the blockchain.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCryptocurrenciesxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aBlockchains (Databases)zUnited States. 0aElectronic funds transfersxLaw and legislation. 0aCryptocurrenciesxLaw and legislation. 0aBusiness, tax, trade & finance 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aLegislative materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263622 aNational Conference of State Legislatures,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8104214240uhttps://www.ncsl.org/financial-services/cryptocurrency-2022-legislation413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20250708081015/https://www.ncsl.org/financial-services/cryptocurrency-2022-legislationzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02599nai a22003257i wotm2024-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xx || d o    |0    2eng d00aLexsitus. 1a[Place of publication not identified] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-11 and 2024-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aLexsitus is an open access online platform for learning and working with legal sources in international criminal law. Users can create reading lists and take notes while working on legal documents. It is developed by the Center for International Law Research and Policy (CILRAP) in partnership with the International Nuremberg Principles Academy, HELM Studio, and Mithya Labs in 2018. Lexsitus offers access to a variety of resources, including lectures, case law, commentary, and digests. The platform is structured around the International Criminal Court’s (ICC) Rome Statute, Rules of Procedure and Evidence, and Elements of Crimes. Lexsitus’s design is based on the ICC’s legal practice, making it useful for both beginners and advanced users.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aInternational criminal law.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2010015367 0aInternational criminal courts.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh94003151 0aInternational criminal law & transitional justice 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aLectures.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402612240uhttps://cilrap-lexsitus.org/en413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260112045043/https://cilrap-lexsitus.org/enzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02383nai a22003857i wotm2024-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1458712231  an-us---00aImmi :bfree and simple information for immigrants.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aImmi 1aSan Francisco :bImmigration Advocates Network  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-10 and 2024-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Immi Website provides immigrants in the U.S. with free resources to understand their legal options. Their platform offers an online screening tool, legal information, and referrals to nonprofit legal services organizations, all available at no cost. Immi was developed by the Immigration Advocates Network and Pro Bono Net, two nonprofit organizations committed to expanding access to justice for low-income immigrants.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aEmigration and immigration lawzUnited States. 0aImmigration enforcementzUnited States. 0aEmigration and immigrationxGovernment policy. 0aImmigration & citizenship 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aUnited States (federal) 6aOrganization website40uhttps://www.immi.org/en413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261029061908/https://www.immi.org/enzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02827nai a22003857i wotm2024-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999oku|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)741497618  an-us-ok00aSovereignty Symposium. 1aOklahoma City, Oklahoma :bOklahoma Supreme Court?  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-09 and 2024-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Sovereignty Symposium was founded in 1988 by Oklahoma Supreme Court Justice Yvonne Kauger to advance the collective understanding of legal principles and doctrines. The Sovereignty Symposium is the premier annual national legal conference focusing on aspects of tribal sovereignty and government to provide critical analyses of contemporary legal issues and propose innovative solutions. The production of the Symposium transitioned to Oklahoma City University and Oklahoma City University School of Law in 2023. This collection includes papers and other materials written by the panelists (faculty) of each year’s Symposium and artwork created for each year’s Symposium.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aIndians of North AmericaxLegal status, laws, etc.zOklahomaxCongresses. 0aIndians of North AmericaxLegal status, laws, etc. 0aIndigenous peoples & tribal law 0aTribal / Indigenous nations 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 5aOklahoma 6aDigital archive / collection2 aOklahoma City University.bSchool of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7700244940uhttps://scholarship.law.okcu.edu/collections/06f55751-718e-41b7-b111-d6a789b9801f413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260224230233/https://scholarship.law.okcu.edu/collections/06f55751-718e-41b7-b111-d6a789b9801fzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02871nai a22003977i wotm2024-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)145145729600aGlobal plastic laws. 1aWashington :bPlastic Pollution Coalition  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-08 and 2024-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Global Plastic Laws Database is the most extensive tool to date to research, track, and visualize plastic legislation that has been passed around the world. The Database tracks legislation across the full life cycle of plastics and organizes these policies according to life cycle categories and key topics. Recognizing the impacts of plastics throughout its full life cycle, this database is organized into nine topics: Design and Reuse, Extended Producer Responsibility, Maritime Sources, Microplastics, Production and Manufacturing, Reduction, Transparency and Traceability, Waste Management, and Waste Trade. The Global Plastic Laws Database is updated regularly, providing a way to monitor and identify emerging trends, solutions, and policy innovations at local, national, and international levels.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aPlasticsxLaw and legislation. 0aPlastics in packagingxLaw and legislation. 0aPlastics industry and tradexLaw and legislation. 0aPlasticsxEnvironmental aspectsxGovernment policy. 0aPollutionxLaw and legislation. 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aInternational / multinational 6aTracker / map / dashboard 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402608140uhttps://www.globalplasticlaws.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260503140236/https://www.globalplasticlaws.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03674nai a22004097i wotm2024-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)144221255400aVirtual Tribunals :bInternational criminal tribunal records (1945-present).1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aVirtual Tribunals 1aStanford, California :bCenter for Human Rights and International Justice  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-07 and 2024-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aA collaboration with the Stanford University Libraries, the Virtual Tribunals project is a major initiative of the Center for Human Rights and International Justice. The scope of the Virtual Tribunals is international criminal tribunal records, from the post-WWII cases through the contemporary tribunals, fully digitized, and rendered searchable through a single online portal. The Virtual Tribunals platform would facilitate public access to the single most comprehensive database of archival material from the tribunals and truth commissions established in the wake of mass atrocities around the globe. The search function in the Virtual Tribunal would make the discovery of multilingual and multimedia materials far easier and more efficient for both legally trained and non-expert users, so these historic collections could have a much wider impact on educational institutions and lay-audiences, including populations directly affected by conflict or living in relevant diaspora communities. This project takes seriously the question of how temporary institutions might leave behind a legacy that will be of lasting value for scholars, experts, and international students, as well as for the people of the post-conflict societies in whose name these tribunals have been pursuing justice.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aInternational criminal lawxHistoryvSources. 0aInternational criminal courtsxHistoryvSources. 0aWar crimesxHistoryvSources. 0aCrimes against humanityxHistoryvSources. 0aInternational criminal law & transitional justice 0aLegal history & historical documents 0aInternational / multinational 6aDigital archive / collection 7aTrial and arbitral proceedings.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110267082 aStanford University.bCenter for Human Rights and International Justice,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no202204971040uhttps://exhibits.stanford.edu/virtual-tribunals413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260729013034/https://exhibits.stanford.edu/virtual-tribunalszBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03231nai a22004337i wotm2024-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nhu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1437521777  an-us---00aUS State Privacy Legislation Tracker. 1aPortsmouth, NH :bInternational Association of Privacy Professionals  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-06 and 2024-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe International Association of Privacy Professionals (IAPP) founded in 2000 is a not-for-profit association. The IAPP Westin Research Center actively tracks the proposed and enacted comprehensive privacy bills from across the U.S. to stay informed of the changing state privacy landscape. This information is compiled into a map, a detailed chart identifying key provisions in the legislation, and links to enacted state comprehensive privacy laws. The chart identifies fourteen provisions that commonly appear in comprehensive privacy laws to break into two categories – consumer rights and business obligations – Although many of the proposed bills will fail to become law, comparing the key provisions helps break down how privacy is developing in the U.S.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aConfidential communicationszUnited States. 0aData protectionxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 7aConfidential communications.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00874701 7aData protectionxLaw and legislation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00887963 7aComputer security.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00872484 7aSecurity systems.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01110883 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aTracker / map / dashboard2 aInternational Association of Privacy Professionals,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200802932540uhttps://iapp.org/resources/article/us-state-privacy-legislation-tracker413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261102220859/https://iapp.org/resources/article/us-state-privacy-legislation-trackerzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02949nai a22003857i wotm2024-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999mau|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1078785565  an-us---00aCaselaw Access Project :b360 years of United States caselaw.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aCaselaw Access Project (CAP) 1aCambridge, Massachusetts :bPresident and Fellows of Harvard College  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-05 and 2024-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Caselaw Access Project (CAP) digitized the entirety of the Harvard Law School Library’s physical collection of American case law available through online and transformed over 40 million pages of U.S. court decision into a dataset of over 6.7 million cases that represent 360 years of U.S. legal history. The scope of CAP includes all state courts, federal courts, and territorial courts for American Samoa, Dakota Territory, Guam, Native American Courts, Navajo Nation, and the Northern Mariana Islands. The earliest case is from 1658, and more volumes are continuously added through 2020. Each volume has been converted into structured, case-level data broken out by majority and dissenting opinion, with human-checked metadata for party names, docket number, citation, and date.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLaw reports, digests, etc.zUnited States. 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aUnited States (federal) 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aCourt decisions and opinions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261722 aHarvard Law School.bLibrary,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8323461640uhttps://case.law/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261028204130/https://case.law/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02693nai a22003737i wotm2024-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999ne || d o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1376264856  ae------00aCore international crimes evidence database (CICED). 1aLuxembourg :bOffice for Official Publications of the European Communities  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-04 and 2024-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aCore International Crimes Evidence Database (CICED) is a unique, tailor-made judicial database set up by Eurojust to preserve, analyze and store evidence of core international crimes (genocide, crimes against humanity and war crimes). CICED enables the Agency to support national judicial authorities in identifying evidence located in another country that may be relevant to their own investigation. This evidence can be used to corroborate individual offences and events or to unveil systematic actions and provide contextual information.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aWar crimeszEuropean Union countries. 0aInternational criminal law & transitional justice 0aInternational / multinational 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aEuropean Union 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260812 aEurojust,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n201301032440uhttps://www.eurojust.europa.eu/publication/core-international-crimes-evidence-database-ciced413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260804191856/https://www.eurojust.europa.eu/publication/core-international-crimes-evidence-database-cicedzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03018nai a22003977i wotm2024-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999enk|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)130909282100aClimate change laws of the world. 1aLondon, UK :bThe London School of Economics and Political Science : Grantham Research Institute on Climate Change and the Environment  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-03 and 2024-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aClimate Change Laws of the World and Climate Change Litigation Databases compile several years of data collection by both the Grantham Research Institute and the Sabin Center, including the collaboration of Grantham Institute with GLOBE International on a series of Climate Legislation Studies. Climate Change Laws of the World includes national-level climate change legislation and policies globally. The database covers climate and climate-related laws, as well as laws and policies promoting low carbon transitions, which reflects the relevance of climate policy in areas including energy, transport, land use, and climate resilience.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aClimatic changesxLaw and legislationvDigests. 0aClimatic changesxLaw and legislationvCases. 0aClimatic changesxGovernment policy. 0aClimatic changesxLaw and legislation. 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aInternational / multinational 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aStatutes and codes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110266112 aGrantham Research Institute on Climate Change and the Environment,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nb20170167852 aSabin Center for Climate Change Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201505923140uhttps://climate-laws.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261020185827/https://climate-laws.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02937nai a22003857i wotm2024-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)141964106900aATIS.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aATIS (Alliance for Telecommunications Industry Solutions) 1aWashington, DC :bAlliance for Telecommunications Industry Solutions  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-02 and 2024-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Alliance for Telecommunications Industry Solutions (ATIS) founded in 1983 is a United States-based organization that develops technical and operational standards for the global telecommunications industry. ATIS has been a key player in the development and implementation of innovative communication technologies and solutions to drive the evolution of networks and services. ATIS is governed by a Board of Directors, which is composed of representatives from leading telecommunications companies, including AT&T, Verizon, T-Mobile and US Cellular, among others. The organization operates through various committees, working groups, and forums, focused on specific areas of interest within the information and communications technology sector.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 7aInformation technologyxLaw and legislation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00973111 7aInformation servicesxStandards.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00972737 7aManagement information systems.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01007271 7aInformation society.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00972767 7aTelecommunicationxLaw and legislation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01145888 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 6aOrganization website2 aAlliance for Telecommunications Industry Solutions,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200504481340uhttps://atis.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261012104306/https://atis.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02922nai a22004337i wotm2024-1220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999ph || w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1416379875  aa-ph---00aTebtebba :bIndigenous Peoples' International Centre for Policy Research and Education.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aTebtebba 1aBaguio City, Philippines :bTebtebba Foundation, Indigenous Peoples' International Centre for Policy Research and Education  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2024).  aFeatured between 2024-01 and 2024-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aTebtebba (Indigenous Peoples’ International Centre for Policy Research and Education) ,established in 1996, is an indigenous peoples’ organization and works for the respect, protection and fulfillment of indigenous peoples’ rights and their self-determined sustainable development. Tebtebba takes the lead in policy advocacy and campaigns on all issues affecting indigenous peoples and seeks to promote and disseminate widely indigenous peoples worldviews, their perspectives on key issues such as individual and collective human rights, sustainable development, climate change, biodiversity, traditional knowledge, customary laws and governance, conflict transformation, gender, etc.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aIndigenous peopleszPhilippines. 0aIndigenous peoplesxLegal status, laws, etc. 0aIndigenous peoplesxGovernment policy. 0aIndigenous peoplesxCivil rights. 0aIndigenous peoples & tribal law 0aHuman rights 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aInternational / multinational 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aPhilippines 6aOrganization website40uhttps://www.tebtebba.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261024011228/https://www.tebtebba.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02404nai a22003617i wotm2025-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999mau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1559713021  an-us---00aH2O :bbuild a better casebook.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aH2O 1aCambridge, Massachusetts :bHarvard Law School  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-11 and 2025-12 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aH2O is a free platform that helps faculty reduce textbook costs for their students, gain greater control over their course materials, and expand the possibilities of open legal education. The open-access casebooks in H2O enable users to create, share, and remix openly licensed casebooks and other course materials. The platform is developed and maintained by the Library Innovation Lab at the Harvard Law School Library. More detailed information can be found here.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLawzUnited StatesvCases. 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 7aCasebooks (Law).2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110261152 aHarvard Law School,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8000125040uhttps://opencasebook.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260925130621/https://opencasebook.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02516nai a22003497i wotm2025-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xx || w o    |0    2eng d  au-nz---00aEssential legal research skills in Aotearoa New Zealand. 1a[Place of publication not identified] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-10 and 2025-11 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis guide to legal research and citation of New Zealand law provides direction to students undertaking legal research. It may also be useful for members of the public and self-represented litigants. It covers the core skills and knowledge for effective legal research, such as creating a search strategy, identifying where to locate legal information and legal citation using the New Zealand Law Style Guide. This text explores searching for sources of legal information including case law, legislation and commentary and explains the different approaches required to locate and cite these sources of law.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLegal researchzNew Zealand. 0aCitation of legal authoritieszNew Zealand. 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aForeign & comparative law 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aNew Zealand 7aHandbooks and manuals.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402610940uhttps://oercollective.caul.edu.au/essential-legal-research-skills-nz/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260518165058/https://oercollective.caul.edu.au/essential-legal-research-skills-nz/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.01923nai a22003257i wotm2025-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999mau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)154304794800aCLBB NeuroLaw Library. 1a[Boston] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-09 and 2025-10 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe CLBB NeuroLaw Library, issued by the Center for Law, Brain and Behavior NeuroLaw Library, provides public access to reliable neuroscience knowledge to promote fairer, more effective, and science-oriented judicial outcomes.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aNeurosciencesxLaw and legislation. 0aCriminal law & criminal justice 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 6aDigital archive / collection40uhttps://clbbneurolawlibrary.com/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260717023758/https://clbbneurolawlibrary.com/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03071nai a22003737i wotm2025-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999wiu|| d o    s0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1534766968  an-us---00a50 constitutions :bexplore state constitutions. 1aMadison, WI :bUniversity of Wisconsin Law School, State Democracy Research Initiative  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-08 and 2025-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  a50 Constitutions, a project of the University of Wisconsin Law School’s State Democracy Research Initiative, seeks to make state constitutions more accessible through a central repository of searchable texts, supplemented by resources and research tools. Their Tracking Constitutional Change feature allows users to see how constitutions have evolved over time and to explore key historical moments. With the redline tool, users can compare a provision’s language across different dates and identify specific textual changes. The State Democracy Research Initiative promotes research and dialogue on state-level democracy, government institutions, and public law nationwide. By centering on the states, the Initiative provides a vital resource for academics, courts, policymakers, advocates, and the public.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aConstitutionszUnited StatesxStates.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2009121607 0aConstitutional lawzUnited StatesxStates.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008101680 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aConstitutions.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2011026167 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260812 aUniversity of Wisconsin--Madison.bLaw School,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8203941640uhttps://50constitutions.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261028035052/https://50constitutions.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02492nai a22003857i wotm2025-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1405202598  an-us---00aState court report (Brennan Center for Justice). 1aNew York :bBrennan Center for Justice  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-07 and 2025-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe State court report, issued by the Brennan Center for Justice in New York, provides public access to legal news, trends, and cutting-edge scholarship. It offers insights and commentary from a nationwide network of academics, journalists, judges, and practitioners with diverse perspectives and expertise.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCourtszUnited StatesxStates.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85033577 0aConstitutional lawzUnited StatesxStates.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008101680 0aLawzUnited StatesxStates. 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aCourts, judiciary & case law 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 7aPeriodicals.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261392 aBrennan Center for Justice,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9700388140uhttps://statecourtreport.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261029101517/https://statecourtreport.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03034nai a22004217i wotm2025-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1526057197  an-us---00aCivil Rights Division. 1aWashington, DC :bU. S. Department of Justice, Civil Rights Division  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-06 and 2025-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aEstablished by the Civil Rights Act of 1957, the Civil Rights Division of the Department of Justice was created to uphold the civil and constitutional rights of all individuals in the United States. Since its founding, the Division has grown significantly in size and scope, playing a key role in many of the nation’s landmark civil rights battles. It enforces federal laws that prohibit discrimination based on race, color, sex, disability, religion, familial status, national origin, and citizenship status. The Division also strives to educate the public about its work and continually improves its website to enhance accessibility and usability. If you would like to share feedback regarding any part of the Division’s website, please share it at crt.overview@usdoj.govcLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCivil rightszUnited StatesvCases. 0aHuman rightszUnited StatesvCases. 7aUnited States Department of Justice Civil Rights Division.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01852811 7aCivil rights.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00862627 7aHuman rights.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00963285 7aTrials.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01156290 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website1 aUnited States.bDepartment of Justice.bCivil Rights Division,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7900900340uhttps://www.justice.gov/crt413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261101212042/https://www.justice.gov/crtzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02468nai a22003737i wotm2025-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xx || w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1521429026  an-us---00aWildland fire. 1aAlbuquerque, New Mexico :bLandscape Partnership  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-05 and 2025-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Landscape Partnership Portal website serves as a clearinghouse to support technical experts, fostering a community of practice. It connects individuals and diverse groups by linking the wildland fire information they maintain on their respective websites, enhancing connectivity and information sharing both within the broader fire community and between the fire community of practice and other landscape conservation practitioners.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aWildfiresxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aNatural resourcesxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aWildfiresxLaw and legislation. 0aNatural resourcesxLaw and legislation. 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aUnited States (federal) 6aPortal or gateway (links to other sites)40uhttps://www.landscapepartnership.org/networks/working-lands-for-wildlife/wildland-fire413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261007222957/https://www.landscapepartnership.org/networks/working-lands-for-wildlife/wildland-firezBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03071nai a22004457i wotm2025-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| d o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1022269930  an-us---00aGovinfo. 1aWashington, D.C :bUnited States Government Printing Office  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-04 and 2025-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aGovInfo is a service of the U.S. Government Publishing Office (GPO), a federal agency within the legislative branch and provides public access to official publications from all three branches of the Federal Government. GPO launched GovInfo in February 2016 to replace its predecessor, the Federal Digital System (FDsys). GovInfo was redesigned to incorporate user feedback and improve search and access to electronic federal government information. The mobile-friendly site uses innovative technologies and offers new features to enhance the overall user experience.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aGovernment publicationszUnited StatesxInformation servicesvDatabases. 0aFederal governmentzUnited StatesxInformation servicesvDatabases. 0aElectronic government informationzUnited StatesxInformation servicesvDatabases. 7aFederal governmentxInformation services.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00922345 7aGovernment publicationsxInformation services.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00945510 7aInformation services.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00972696 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aUnited States (federal) 6aGovernment agency website 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260811 aUnited States.bGovernment Publishing Office,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201500250340uhttps://www.govinfo.gov/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261106041211/https://www.govinfo.gov/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02347nai a22003737i wotm2025-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999cau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)176634973  an-us---00aGovspeak :ba guide to government acronyms & abbreviations. 1aLa Jolla, CA :bUC San Diego Library  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-03 and 2025-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Govspeak website, researched and compiled by Subject Specialist Librarian Kelly L. Smith at the UC San Diego Library, lists acronyms and abbreviations commonly used by the United States federal government. Each entry is defined and includes a link to the homepage of its corresponding institution.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aAdministrative agencieszUnited StatesvDirectories. 0aGovernment informationzUnited StatesvDirectories. 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aUnited States (federal) 7aAbbreviation dictionaries.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2025026126 7aDirectories.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402608740uhttps://ucsd.libguides.com/govspeak413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20140706060438/http://libguides.ucsd.edu/govspeakzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03163nai a22003977i wotm2025-1020261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| d o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)45008224  an-us---00aCatalog of U.S. government publications.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aCatalog of U.S. Government Publications (CGP) 1aWashington, D.C :bU. S. Government Printing Office, ǂc 2006-  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-02 and 2025-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Catalog of U.S. Government Publications (CGP) is the search tool for print and electronic titles of the National Bibliography of U.S. Government Publications. The National Bibliography includes publications from across the legislative, executive, and judicial branches of the U.S. Government. The CGP is a bibliography, not a document repository, and provides links to full-text resources that are the official, authoritative copy of an electronic resource. The current CGP came online in 2006. The CGP was originally the online counterpart of the Monthly Catalog of United States Government Publications. Commonly referred to as the Monthly Catalog, or MoCat. The online catalog lists all of the publications produced by the U.S. Government Printing Office from 1895 to 2004. The MoCat was discontinued with the December 2004 edition; now the continuously-updated CGP serves as the official record.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 7aGovernment publications.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00945492 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aLegal research & reference tools 0aUnited States (federal) 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aBibliographies.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140260481 aUnited States.bGovernment Publishing Office,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no201500250340uhttps://catalog.data.gov/organization/gpo413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261110211120/https://catalog.data.gov/organization/gpozBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02567nai a22004217i wotm2025-1120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999gau|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1482738117  an-us---00aVaccination laws. 1aAtlanta, GA :b: Centers for Disease Control and Prevention  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2025).  aFeatured between 2025-01 and 2025-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Vaccination Laws website, compiled by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) in partnership with other organizations, provides vaccination requirements and regulations to support and enhance vaccine safety.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aVaccinationxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aPublic health lawszUnited States. 0aVaccine mandatesxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aVaccinesxGovernment policyzUnited States. 0aVaccinationxLaw and legislation. 0aVaccinesxGovernment policy. 0aHealth, disability & social welfare 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 6aGovernment agency website 7aStatutes and codes.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110266112 aCenters for Disease Control and Prevention (U.S.),eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no9301152940uhttps://www.cdc.gov/phlp/php/publications/vaccination-laws.html/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261012143252/https://www.cdc.gov/phlp/php/publications/vaccination-laws.html/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02282nai a22003737i wotm2026-0120261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999txu|| w o    s0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)47014739  an-us---00aCybercemetery. 1aDenton, TX :bUniversity of North Texas, Government Documents Department  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2026).  aFeatured 2026-09 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aCyberCemetery is an archive of government websites that have ceased operation, primarily websites of defunct government agencies and commissions that have issued a final report. The collection covers a wide range of topics, reflecting the broad scope of government information.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aGovernment informationzUnited StatesvDatabases. 0aElectronic government informationzUnited StatesvDatabases. 0aGovernment Web siteszUnited States. 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aUnited States (federal) 6aDigital archive / collection2 aUniversity of North Texas.bLibraries,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/no200104286240uhttps://govinfo.library.unt.edu/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261017153239/https://govinfo.library.unt.edu/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02874nai a22003617i wotm2026-0220261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| d o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1609552277  an-us---00aCopyright Public Records Portal. 1aWashington, D.C :bU.S. Copyright Office  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2026).  aFeatured between 2026-07 and 2026-08 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe Copyright Public Records Portal is the online search system provided by the United States Copyright Office that allows the public to search copyright registration and recordation records maintained by the U.S. Copyright Office. The portal allows users to: - Search copyright registration records. - Search recorded documents, such as copyright transfers, assignments, and licenses. - Locate information about copyright owners, authors, claimants, titles, and registration numbers. - Access newer records through a modern search interface For librarians and legal researchers, the portal serves as a starting point for identifying copyright registrations and ownership information maintained by the U.S. Copyright Office. It is particularly useful when researching the copyright status of published works or tracing ownership through recorded transfers.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aCopyrightzUnited States. 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 0aUnited States (federal) 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aRecords (Documents).2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20140261632 aLibrary of Congress.bCopyright Office,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n7911797140uhttps://www.copyright.gov/public-records/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261030224410/https://www.copyright.gov/public-records/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03515nai a22004457i wotm2026-0320261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999enk|| d o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)160168503500aNon-Human Rights Database. 1a[London] :b[publisher not identified]  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2026).  aFeatured between 2026-06 and 2026-07 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, place of publication, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis database compiles legislation and case law from international and local jurisdictions that recognize rights for non-human entities, including animals, rivers, ecosystems, AI, and machines. It also includes cases in which non-human entities have been granted special legal status, even when full legal rights have not been formally recognized. The database is organized by resource type (legislation and judicial decisions) as well as by jurisdiction (international and local). These categories are further subdivided to facilitate ease of use and navigation. Most resources are presented in their original language, and English translations are provided whenever available. When original legislation, case law, or project documents cannot be obtained, alternative sources-such as press articles-are included to provide relevant context. Although still a work in progress, the database aspires to serve as an expanding resource for legal professionals, scholars, and activists engaged in the emerging field of non-human rights.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aNatural resourcesxLaw and legislation. 0aAutomationxLaw and legislation. 0aAnimalsxLaw and legislation. 0aTechnological innovationsxLaw and legislation. 0aArtificial intelligencexLaw and legislation. 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aConstitutional law & civil rights 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 0aInternational / multinational 0aForeign (single country or region) 5aMultiple countries 7aDatabases.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf2014026081 7aLaw materials.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf20110263512 aUniversity of London.bQueen Mary.bSchool of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/nb201100055340uhttps://www.qmul.ac.uk/decentering-human/non-human-rights-data-law/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260702000438/https://www.qmul.ac.uk/decentering-human/non-human-rights-data-law/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02722nai a22003737i wotm2026-0420261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999xx || w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1593953835  an-us---00aWhat Congress Should Be Reading. 1aSan Francisco, California :bSubstack  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2026).  aFeatured between 2026-05 and 2026-06 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aWhat Congress Should Be Reading website tracks newly released Congressional Research Service (CRS) reports and related materials, providing plain-language summaries, context, and analysis to help the public better understand the information prepared for Congress. CRS reports are produced by the nonpartisan research arm of the Library of Congress to inform Members of Congress and their staff. Although CRS materials have been publicly available since 2018, it remains difficult for the general public to identify newly released reports or understand their significance without sustained monitoring. This publication helps address that gap by regularly highlighting newly issued or updated reports and offering concise synopses so readers can quickly identify topics relevant to their interests.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aPolitical planningzUnited States. 0aNational securityzUnited States. 0aUnited StatesxForeign relations. 0aLegislation & legislative process 0aGovernment information & administrative law 0aUnited States (federal) 7aNewsletters.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402613140uhttps://crsreports.substack.com/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261028075250/https://crsreports.substack.com/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02472nai a22003617i wotm2026-0520261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999mau|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)158848702600aTruth and Reconciliation Commissions. 1aBoston, Massachusetts :bNortheastern University School of Law Center for Global Law and Justice  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2026).  aFeatured between 2026-04 and 2026-05 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: subject headings, name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis resource, created by Northeastern University School of Law, provides public access to information about widespread human rights violations by investigating abuses, documenting systemic patterns of violence, uncovering the fate of victims, and promoting accountability.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aTruth commissions.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2001002255 0aTransitional justice.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2008008884 0aInternational criminal law & transitional justice 0aHuman rights 0aInternational / multinational 6aResearch guide (web)2 aNortheastern University (Boston, Mass.). School of Law,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8580045940uhttps://cglj.org/human-rights-oversight/national-systems/truth-and-reconciliation-commissions/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260515022326/https://cglj.org/human-rights-oversight/national-systems/truth-and-reconciliation-commissions/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02456nai a22003977i wotm2026-0620261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999dcu|| w o    f0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)62587897  an-us---00aTravel.state.gov. 1aWashington, D.C :bU.S. Department of State  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2026).  aFeatured between 2026-03 and 2026-04 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aTravel.state.gov is a website created by the U.S. Department of State, Bureau of Consular Affairs, which provides practical information for various topics that affect American citizens who are dealing with legal and other issues aboard and legal resources and judicial assistance information such as how to find an attorney in the foreign jurisdiction.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aInternational travel regulationsxComputer network resources. 0aIntercountry adoptionxComputer network resources. 0aTravelxComputer network resources. 0aImmigration & citizenship 0aGender, sexuality, family & children 0aUnited States (federal) 0aInternational / multinational 6aGovernment agency website1 aUnited States.bBureau of Consular Affairs,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8002453540uhttps://travel.state.gov/content/travel.html413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260624201511/https://travel.state.gov/content/travel.htmlzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02648nai a22004097i wotm2026-0720261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1406155590  an-us---00aTrademark law :ban open-source casebook. 1aNew York :bNew York University  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2026).  aFeatured between 2026-02 and 2026-03 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThis resource provides public access to the Trade Law casebook, beginning with The History of U.S. Trademark Law and The Origins of Trademarks and Trademark Law, highlighting how the early development of trademark law raises questions that continue to concern courts and policymakers–particularly regarding the proper rationale for trademark protection and the scope of trademark rights.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aTrademarksxLaw and legislationzUnited States. 0aTrademark searchingzUnited States. 0aTrademarkszUnited StatesvCases. 7aTrademark searching.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01153891 7aTrademarks.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01153892 7aTrademarksxLaw and legislation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01153903 7aUnited States.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01204155 0aIntellectual property, technology & privacy 0aUnited States (federal) 7aCasebooks (Law).2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201102611540uhttps://www.tmcasebook.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/BeebeTMLaw-v10-digital-edition.pdf413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20240719214101/https://www.tmcasebook.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/BeebeTMLaw-v10-digital-edition.pdfzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.02845nai a22004337i wotm2026-0820261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999nyu|| w o    |0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)1571474606  an-us---00aLawHelp.org :bhelping people find solutions to their legal problems.1 iTitle as listed by LSRD-SIS:aLawHelp.org 1aNew York, NY :bPro Bono Net  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2026).  aFeatured between 2026-01 and 2026-02 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: geographic area codes, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aLawHelp is a program of Pro Bono Net, a national nonprofit dedicated to bringing the power of the law to all. LawHelp.org was created to help people without lawyers understand their rights, make informed decisions, and connect with help in their local communities. LawHelp.org provides referrals to nonprofit legal aid organizations in every state and territory, free legal rights resources, court forms, and self-advocacy tools. The site also includes a network of 20 statewide legal information portals developed using the LawHelp platform, and links to other trusted sources of legal help.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aPublic interest lawzUnited States. 0aLegal aidxComputer network resources. 0aLegal assistance to the poorxComputer network resources. 0aLawzUnited StatesxComputer network resources. 0aLawzUnited StatesvCases. 0aLawxComputer network resources. 0aLegal profession, education & access to justice 0aUnited States (state, local or multi-state) 0aUnited States (federal) 6aPortal or gateway (links to other sites) 7aDirectories.2lcgft0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/genreForms/gf201402608740uhttps://www.lawhelp.org/413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20261013135621/https://www.lawhelp.org/zBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.03143nai a22004577i wotm2026-0920261110224125.0m     o  d        cr |n|||||||||261110cuuuu9999it || w o    i0    2eng d  a(OCoLC)156507533200aPeople-Centred Land Governance for Peace, Security and Stability :bExploring Rule of Law Solutions. 1aRome, Italy :bInternational Development Law Organization IDLO  a1 online resource  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  aSelected as an LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month (section archive listing: 2026).  aFeatured between 2025-12 and 2026-01 (estimated from dated archive sources).  aTitle, publication and subject data from the University of Hawaiʻi Law Library catalog record.  aMachine-assigned by Claude (Anthropic), November 2026: name headings, 008 codes, access note. Name, subject and code values were checked against id.loc.gov; not reviewed by a cataloger.0 aUnrestricted online accessfUnrestricted online access2star  aThe publication frames people-centred land governance not just as a technical land administration issue but as a rule of law and peacebuilding priority – one that strengthens social cohesion, reduces drivers of conflict, and supports sustainable development pathways. It offers practical policy recommendations to governments, international partners and civil society for advancing these objectives.cLSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month  aDescription based on the LSRD-SIS Legal Website of the Month archive entry; site address verified 2026-11-10; resource not examined by the cataloger. 0aLand tenurexGovernment policyzDeveloping countries. 0aLand reformxLaw and legislationzDeveloping countries. 0aLand usexGovernment policyzDeveloping countries. 0aSustainable developmentzDeveloping countries. 7aLand tenurexGovernment policy.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00991376 7aLand reformxLaw and legislation.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00991288 7aLand usexGovernment policy.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00991504 7aCultural relativism.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00885057 7aHuman rights.2fast0(OCoLC)fst00963285 7aDeveloping countries.2fast0(OCoLC)fst01242969 0aEnvironmental, energy & natural resources law 0aHuman rights 0aInternational / multinational 6aReport or study (single document)2 aInternational Development Law Organization,eissuing body.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n201021746240uhttps://www.idlo.int/publications/people-centred-land-governance-peace-security-and-stability-exploring-rule-law413Archived versionuhttps://web.archive.org/web/20260512163000/https://www.idlo.int/publications/people-centred-land-governance-peace-security-and-stability-exploring-rule-lawzBackup copy in the Internet Archive Wayback Machine.